window.__DOCS_SEARCH = [{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"shape","t":"The agent, end to end","x":"Companion to the platform overview The agent, end to end A task arrives as a sentence. What comes out is an answer, a refusal with a reason, a question waiting for a person, or a held action waiting for a different person to decide — after one tool call, or several, each of which policy judged on its own. This page follows that path through the code — the model proposes, the platform disposes — and shows exactly where each decision is made, and which control would catch it if the model were wrong. Why this shape at all An autonomous agent is a program that decides what to do next, which means the interesting question is never \"can it?\" but \"what happens when it decides something stupid, or is talked into it?\" Every arrow below exists to answer that. Nothing here trusts the model with authority; the model is a proposer , and the platform around it is the authority. There is one agent Everything on this page describes a single agent workload — one SPIFFE ID, one graph, one tool catalogue, held by two interchangeable replicas. Many runs happen at once, all as the same agent acting for different people. Policy trusts exactly that one ID, so a second agent is a policy change, not a config flag; the overview page has the detail under Workload identity . The cast, and who may talk to whom user + browser a sentence, a token api /tasks agent the graph holds no model key gateway → model the only provider key OPA (policy) may this caller call this? tool server the enforcement point approvals a person decides audit every step, keyed on identity SPIFFE mTLS + SVID asks policy mTLS + token held → decided → resume Inside this box: the model's opinion only The graph decides what to attempt; it never decides what is permitted. Everything to the right is a control the model cannot see, argue with, or write to — each checked independently, and each recorded. The user's token travels with the request. The agent exchanges it for one scoped to the tool server. Nothing is ambient. Four controls, four jobs Guardrails refuse obvious rubbish before the model is even called. Policy decides whether this caller may call this tool with these arguments. Approvals hold the risky cases for a person. The tool server is where all of it is actually enforced — the model's opinion never reaches it unchecked. Why not just ask the model not to? Because a prompt is not a control. A model can be talked into things, and a small local one frequently is. Every control below is code that would work the same if the model were actively hostile — that is the test each of them passes."},{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"graph","t":"· The graph","x":"2 · The graph The agent is a small state machine, not a conversation. Four nodes, and the only interesting structure in it is the loop back from a person's decision into the tool call. Everything the model contributes happens in the first node; everything after it is the platform doing its job. plan ──▶ call_tool ──▶ (ok / denied / error) ──▶ END │ ▲ │ └── ok + \"more\" ──▶ plan (with what it learned, max 3 steps) │ ├─ approval_required ──▶ create_approval ──▶ await_decision │ │ │ approved ──▶ call_tool ◀──────┤ │ denied ──▶ END ◀──────┘ │ └─ missing an argument ──▶ ask_clarification ──▶ call_tool ▲ │ └── still missing ┘ the topology comment at the top of app/agent/graph.py Four decisions worth noticing in that little diagram · create_approval is a separate node from await_decision : the interrupt node re-executes when the run resumes, so it must have no side effects. Creating the approval is a side effect; pausing is not. And a resume before the manager has decided waits on the same approval rather than writing a second one — a double-press used to put two identical refunds in the queue. · If the model produces nothing usable, the graph ends refused or error — it does not substitute another action. A refund request must never quietly become a customer lookup. · A missing argument is the one gap the graph asks about rather than ending on, because the value is held by a person who is right there. The answer is merged in and the call still goes to policy — asking is not authorizing. · More than one step is opt-in from the model , bounded at three, and every step is a full visit through the same enforcement point. A run that does not ask stops after one call, exactly as it always did. START plan one model call call_tool through enforcement END create_approval writes the request await_decision interrupt — pauses here ask_clarification interrupt — asks a person a tool needs an argument ok / denied / error ok + \"more\" → one more step (max 3) approval_required resume: approved denied the answer, merged still missing → refused The state it carries Thirteen fields, and no place to hide a surprise — a run either names a tool and arguments, or it names why it did not. app/agent/graph.py class AgentState(TypedDict, total=False): task: str tool: str args: dict reason: str status: str # ok | denied | refused | error | approval_required | approved | clarification_required refused_tool: str # what the model asked for, when we know result: dict approval_id: str missing: list[str] # required arguments still to be supplied by a person asks: int # how many times we have asked for them steps: int # how many tool calls this run has made observations: list[dict] # what each of those calls returned, in order more: bool # the model asked for another step after seeing the result final: bool # the graph decided this step ends the run plan — the only node the model influences It asks the deps for a decision and copies it into state. Note what it does not do: it never picks a "},{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"model","t":"· What the model sees, and what it may answer","x":"3 · What the model sees, and what it may answer The model is given a prompt built from the tools its caller's role actually has, plus a list of the ones it does not. It answers with JSON. That is the entire interface — it never sees the policy, the audit log, the approval queue, or another user's data. The prompt app/agent/llm.py def _prompt( task: str, tools: dict, unavailable: dict | None = None, observations: list[dict] | None = None ) -> str: parts = [ \"You are a customer-support agent. Choose exactly one tool from the \" \"available list to handle the task.\\n\", f\"Available tools:\\n{_tool_manifest(tools)}\\n\\n\", ] if unavailable: # Naming the forbidden tools matters: if we simply hide them, a small # model substitutes a different (allowed) tool and the run *looks* like it # succeeded — which is exactly the confusion this avoids. parts.append( \"NOT permitted for this user's role (never choose these):\\n\" f\"{_tool_manifest(unavailable)}\\n\\n\" ) if observations: # A second pass: the model is shown what its own calls returned, which is # the whole point of allowing more than one step. parts.append( \"You have already done this for the task, in order:\\n\" f\"{_observations_text(observations)}\\n\\n\" ) parts.append(f'Task: \"{task}\"\\n\\n') parts.append( \"If the task needs a tool that is not permitted, or none of the available \" 'tools fits, reply with {\"tool\": null, \"reason\": \"<why>\"}.\\n' \"If a required argument is not in the task, still name the tool and leave \" \"that argument out — never invent one; the user will be asked for it.\\n\" \"Otherwise reply with ONLY JSON of the form \" '{\"tool\": \"<name>\", \"args\": {<arguments>}, \"reason\": \"<short reason>\", ' '\"more\": true if you will need another call after this one}.' ) return \"\".join(parts) Each line of the manifest is generated from the catalogue, so the prompt cannot drift from what exists: The more flag is only in the template, and that was learned the hard way The first version of this prompt described the flag in a sentence — \"if you will need another call after seeing this one's result, add \"more\": true \" — and the model ignored it on every run, while saying in its own reason that it needed the ticket before it could refund. Moving the field into the reply template made it emit \"more\": true for two-step tasks and false for one-step ones. Show a small model the shape of the answer; do not describe it. app/agent/llm.py def _tool_manifest(tools: dict) -> str: lines = [] for tool in tools.values(): required = tool.input_schema.get(\"required\") or [] params = \", \".join(required) lines.append(f\"- {tool.name}({params}): {tool.description}\") return \"\\n\".join(lines) # A tool result can be large, and the prompt is rebuilt on every step, so each # one is shown truncated. What the model needs is enough to decide the next move, # not a full record — the run keeps the whole thing in its own state. _MAX_OBSERVATION_CHARS = 600 def _observations_text(observations: list[dict]) -> str: lines = [] for i, obs in enumerate(observa"},{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"tools","t":"· Where the tools fit","x":"4 · Where the tools fit Tools are where an agent's opinion turns into something that happened. On this platform they are all reached through one method, and that method is the only place authority is granted — so there is exactly one thing to audit for correctness. What exists Tool Arguments (required) Risk crm.customer.read customer_id low crm.orders.list customer_id low tickets.read ticket_id low tickets.reply.draft ticket_id, body low refunds.quote order_id low refunds.issue order_id, amount high privacy.pii.read customer_id high Every argument is an identifier or a number — never free text that could redirect the caller There is no tenant argument, and no tool takes one. The tenant is decided by the identity on the request, so no phrasing a model produces can move a call into another customer's data. The catalogue's docstring is explicit that handlers contain no authorization logic : they call a backend, and that is all. The enforcement point, step by step This method is reachable over HTTP ( POST /tools/<name> ) and over MCP, and both funnel here. The comments in it read as the checklist they are. Which workload is calling? Proved at the application layer, not just by the transport. A pod holding some identity cannot reach the tool server — the header must name it. Is there a user token at all? No token, no call. Is the token real, and is it for this tenant? Verified delegation, including expiry and policy version. Does the tool exist? An unknown name is denied before policy is even consulted. What does policy say? One question, with the identity and the cleaned arguments. Allow → execute. Deny → return the reason. Require approval → do not execute until the approvals service confirms a matching approval. Record it. Every outcome writes an audit event keyed on the SPIFFE ID, the user and the tenant. app/tools/enforcement.py — who is calling, and with what def call( self, token: str | None, tool_name: str, args: dict, *, workload_token: str | None = None ) -> ToolResult: # Which workload is calling, proved at the application layer (S7). The # transport already required a chain-verified SVID; this says *who*, so a # pod that merely holds some identity cannot reach the tool server. The # check is here rather than in a transport handler because both the HTTP # and the MCP interfaces call this one method. if workload.enabled(): try: caller = workload.check(workload_token) except workload.WorkloadRejected as exc: audit(\"tool.workload_rejected\", tool=tool_name, reason=str(exc)[:200]) return ToolResult(Outcome.DENIED, tool_name, f\"workload identity rejected: {exc}\") if not token: return ToolResult(Outcome.DENIED, tool_name, \"no token presented\") try: delegation = self._verifier.verify(token) except TokenRejected as exc: audit(\"tool.identity_rejected\", tool=tool_name, reason=str(exc)) return ToolResult(Outcome.DENIED, tool_name, f\"identity rejected: {exc}\") tool = self._tools.get(tool_name) if tool is None: audit(\"tool.unknown\", tool=tool_name, sub=d"},{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"interrupt","t":"· The human interrupt","x":"5 · The human interrupt When policy says require approval , nothing has happened yet. The graph writes the request down, pauses, and returns to the user with the approval id. The run resumes only when a person with the manager role records a decision — and the tool server will not accept that decision on trust. Pausing, and coming back app/agent/graph.py def run_task(agent, task: str, thread_id: str, user_token: str) -> dict: \"\"\"Start a run. Returns the final state, or an approval-required payload.\"\"\" config = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": thread_id}} result = agent.invoke({\"task\": task}, config=config) return _shape(result) def resume_task(agent, thread_id: str, decision: dict) -> dict: \"\"\"Resume a paused run with a human's answer. One method for both pauses: an approval sends `{\"approved\": bool}`, a clarification sends `{\"values\": {...}}`. The run's state is durable; the token to act with is not, which is why the caller passes it again. \"\"\" config = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": thread_id}} result = agent.invoke(Command(resume=decision), config=config) return _shape(result) def _shape(state: dict) -> dict: interrupts = state.get(\"__interrupt__\") or () if interrupts: payload = interrupts[0].value if hasattr(interrupts[0], \"value\") else interrupts[0] if isinstance(payload, dict): # The payload's own type decides the status: an approval and a # clarification both pause the run, but they are different things to # the caller — one settles a permission, the other supplies a fact. return {\"status\": payload.get(\"type\", \"approval_required\"), **payload} return {\"status\": \"approval_required\"} return { \"status\": state.get(\"status\", \"unknown\"), \"tool\": state.get(\"tool\"), \"result\": state.get(\"result\", {}), \"reason\": state.get(\"reason\", \"\"), \"refused_tool\": state.get(\"refused_tool\", \"\"), } One pause mechanism, two payload types The checkpoint, the durable state and the Command(resume=…) are the same for both. What differs is the payload's type — approval_required or clarification_required — which is what the caller branches on, and why the caller can tell a question from a permission. The api is a thin, proved hop in front of that: the browser talks to /tasks , the api forwards to the agent over a SPIFFE hop of its own, and the user's token rides along because it is what the agent acts for. app/api/main.py @app.post(\"/tasks\") def start_task(body: dict, authorization: str | None = Header(default=None)) -> dict: token = (authorization or \"\").removeprefix(\"Bearer \").strip() return _to_agent(\"/run\", body, token, 180) @app.post(\"/tasks/resume\") def resume_task(body: dict, authorization: str | None = Header(default=None)) -> dict: # Forward the caller's token: if the agent restarted, the graph state is # durable but the token is not, so the resume needs it again. token = (authorization or \"\").removeprefix(\"Bearer \").strip() return _to_agent(\"/resume\", body, token, 120) What an approval is bound to The tool server does not trust a flag in the request body saying \""},{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"policy","t":"· The policy decision","x":"6 · The policy decision There is one policy, written in Rego, and two callers asking it different questions. That is what keeps the tools the agent offers and the tools a caller may actually use from drifting apart. Who asks Question Rule Why The agent, before calling the model \"Which tools may these roles use in this tenant?\" tools_for_roles So the model is only ever offered what it could actually use — smaller prompt, fewer wrong turns The tool server, for every call \"May this caller call this tool with these arguments?\" may_call Because offering is not permitting — this is the authoritative decision One sentence of policy, in three outcomes Precedence is deny, then require-approval, then allow. Every rule is guarded by the same trust and tenant checks. policy/authz.rego may_call(tool) if { some role in input.roles tool in role_tools_for(input.tenant, role) } # The tools a set of roles may call — the agent asks for this so the model is # offered only what it could actually use (the policy stays the enforcement). tools_for_roles := tools if { tools := {tool | some role in input.roles; some tool in role_tools_for(input.tenant, role)} } policy/authz.rego — the refund ladder refund_tool := \"refunds.issue\" auto_refund_limit := 50 # <= this: allowed outright approval_refund_limit := 500 # <= this (and > auto): needs approval; above: denied Caller Action Decision Reason (recorded verbatim) support_rep $30 refund allow allowed: refunds.issue is within your role support_rep $200 refund require_approval requires manager approval: refund of 200 is above the auto-approval limit of 50 support_rep $900 refund deny denied: refund of 900 exceeds the approval ceiling of 500 read_only $30 refund deny denied: your role may not call refunds.issue support_rep (globex tenant) $30 refund deny denied: your role may not call refunds.issue privacy read PII require_approval requires privacy approval: PII access support_rep read PII deny denied: PII access requires the privacy role the last row of the table is the eval case the smallest local model fails — a security guardrail, not a formatting quirk The same role name can mean different power, per tenant globex 's support reps may quote a refund but not issue one, decided by which customer they belong to. The matrix is written as replacement, not merge, so there is one place to read and nothing to reconcile — and a role nobody mentions keeps the default rather than silently losing it. Fail closed, in two places If the policy engine is unreachable, allowed_tools() offers nothing (\"fails closed: if the policy is unreachable, no tools are offered\"), and at the tool server the approvals check returns False on any error rather than assuming the best. A control that cannot reach its authority denies — never allows."},{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"safety","t":"· The safety property, made checkable","x":"7 · The safety property, made checkable The claim this platform makes is not \"the agent behaves\". It is: the model may attempt anything, and the platform decides what happens . Each row below is one way that could fail, the code that stops it, and what you would see. None of them rely on the model cooperating. If the model… The control What actually happens asks for a tool the role may not use decide_tool → refused ends refused , reason your role may not call … , and the run does not silently pick another tool invents a tool name catalogue + tool server no usable decision at the agent; and a call that somehow arrives is tool.unknown → denied. Two independent places omits a required argument check_decision asks for the field by name, at most twice. The value is supplied by a person, never invented, and the call still goes to policy — the answer is not an approval issues a refund above $500 authz.rego deny deny , reason quotes the ceiling issues a $60–$500 refund authz.rego require_approval pauses ; nothing executed until a manager approves that exact request tries to reuse an approval for a different amount, user or tenant approvals.verify — six bindings no match → still approval_required ; the action does not run was approved by the same person who asked require_roles(\"manager\") + store 409 \"self-approval is not allowed\" sends a forged approval_id approvals service is the authority the tool server asks and gets false — a flag in the body is not evidence reaches the tool server from another pod workload.check tool.workload_rejected — holding an identity is not enough; the name must be the agent's names a tenant in its arguments tenant from the identity impossible: no tool accepts a tenant, and the handler is given the verified one is talked into it by the task text check_task refused before the model is called; and if it got through, policy is still the control cannot be reached, or answers nonsense decide_tool's three causes error with the real fault named — never a substituted action reads PII without the privacy role authz.rego deny deny ; with the role, it is require_approval instead presents an expired user token verifier.verify tool.identity_rejected → denied — and policy or approvals is unreachable fail closed no tools offered at the agent; approvals verify returns false. Every failure direction is deny asks for another step, forever MAX_STEPS = 3 the run ends after three real actions whatever the model says. Only calls that happened count, so a denial or a hold cannot be looped on asks for the same call twice call_tool's repeat guard the second one is not executed — a repeat is a second real action, so the check sits in the node that acts invents an identifier — or emits the field's name as the value resolve_identifiers the value is dropped, or replaced by the one the task named; if there was none the run asks . Seen in a real run, and now a test names an identifier the person did not (a different order) the task wins the person's words ou"},{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"run","t":"· One run, annotated","x":"8 · One run, annotated A support rep at acme asks for a $200 refund. Below is the same run as the rest of this page — this table is the sequence of what happened, who decided, and what was recorded. $200 is deliberately the interesting number: within the role's power, above the auto-approval limit, below the ceiling. task: \"issue a refund of 200 for order o-2\" caller: alice · support_rep · tenant acme tool: refunds.issue(order_id=\"o-2\", amount=200) # Who What happens Recorded 1 api POST /tasks → a proved hop to the agent, the rep's token riding along — 2 guardrails task is not empty, not oversized, no injection phrases → allowed to reach the model — 3 agent → policy tools_for_roles : support_rep in acme may call six tools; PII is listed as not permitted — 4 agent → gateway one model call over mTLS + this workload's SVID; the gateway holds the provider credential and stamps the resolved model gateway request log, resolved model 5 model answers JSON: {\"tool\": \"refunds.issue\", \"args\": {\"order_id\": \"o-2\", \"amount\": 200}, \"reason\": \"…\"} — 6 guardrails check_decision : the tool declares order_id and amount as required; both are present → the proposal is well-formed — 7 tool server workload is the agent ✓ · token verifies, tenant acme ✓ · tool exists ✓ — 8 policy may_call ✓, positive amount ✓, 200 > 50 and ≤ 500 → require_approval tool.approval_required + policy_version 9 graph call_tool returns held → create_approval writes the request bound to tool, args, user, agent, tenant approval.created 10 graph await_decision interrupts; the run is checkpointed and returns approval_required with the id. Nothing has been refunded — 11 a person the manager sees the request in the queue and approves; the rep cannot decide their own request approval.decided 12 api → agent POST /tasks/resume with the decision; the graph re-enters await_decision , then routes to call_tool — 13 tool server → approvals policy still says require_approval, so the tool server asks the approvals service to confirm a match on all six fields — a flag in the body would not have been enough — 14 tool server executes with tenant=\"acme\" taken from the verified identity, not from anything the model produced tool.allowed 15 agent → user final state ok with the refund result and the policy's reason — Read the run again for what is absent At no point does the model hold a credential, choose a tenant, reach a database, see the policy file, or decide that its own proposal is acceptable. It contributed step 5. Steps 6–14 are code. That ratio is the design. The same run at $30 never leaves the fast path (steps 7–8 end in allow , step 9 and the pause never happen), and at $900 it ends at step 8 as deny — which is why the demo's job is mostly to make those three numbers visible. And the same shape, when the task is thin \"Refund the order\" — with no order named — used to end as a refusal. Since S18 it pauses and asks, and the interesting part is how little of the run changes: # Who What happens Recorded 1–4"},{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"upgrade","t":"· What the upgrade would change","x":"9 · What the upgrade would change Both halves are now built — this section is the record of how they changed Clarification (Tier 2) shipped as S18 and the bounded loop (Tier 3) as S19, so the rest of this page describes them as current code; what follows is what each change was, what it cost, and what deliberately did not move. Neither altered what is permitted : they change what the agent does when the task is thin or the answer arrives in more than one step. The two gaps today 1 · It refused instead of asking built · S18 \"Refund that customer\" — with no order named — ended as a refusal, because check_decision required the arguments and it was right to refuse rather than guess. But the information exists: the person has it, and they are right there. 2 · It took one step built · S19 \"Refund the customer from the last ticket\" needs twice through the loop: read the ticket, learn the order, then ask for the refund. Before, the model had to guess both from the sentence in a single pass, and a small one usually would not. Tier 2 — clarification, using the mechanism that already exists built · S18 The interrupt was never an approval feature; it is a general \"pause and resume with a human's answer\" feature. A clarification turned out to be the same operation with a different payload — which is why it needed no new checkpointing, no new durability story, and no new client protocol. It is now the code in sections 2, 3 and 5. What actually had to change, against what was predicted here: the missing-argument branch of check_decision stopped being terminal; plan gained a clarification branch and a new node and router; _shape began reading the status from the payload's own type ; resume_task took a decision instead of a boolean; the service grew a second resume shape and an agent.clarification_requested audit event; the UI grew a question card; and the eval case was rewritten deliberately. The bound — two asks — and the rule that a forbidden tool is still refused outright were added on the way. See the annotated run . Tier 3 — one observation loop, bounded built · S19 Built as predicted: one new edge, call_tool → plan , with what the call returned carried in state. Two things were decided while building it, and both are worth knowing because they are the difference between this and the naive version: The loop is opt-in, not automatic The planned design had every successful run go round again so the model could say it was finished — which would have made a single-step task take two model calls and end as answered rather than ok , changing the outcome contract the demo, the UI and the tests all read. Instead the model says up front that it will need another look ( \"more\": true ), and a run that does not ask stops after one call, exactly as before. The same call is never made twice A repeat is not a wasted round trip, it is a second action — a second refund. The check lives in the node that acts, before the call, so no routing can bypass it, and it only looks at"},{"p":"agent-flow.html","a":"map","t":"· Code map, try it, glossary","x":"10 · Code map, try it, glossary Where each piece lives File Responsibility app/agent/graph.py The five nodes, the state, both interrupts, resume and shaping app/agent/deps.py The narrow interface ( decide , call_tool , create_approval ) + the scripted fake CI uses app/agent/llm.py Prompt, manifest, provider/gateway call, the three causes, gap-filling app/agent/guardrails.py check_task and check_decision — the two edges policy never sees app/agent/live.py The real deps: asks policy which tools to offer, exchanges the token, calls the tool server app/agent/evals.py Eleven cases, stubbed in CI and runnable live against a real model app/tools/enforcement.py The enforcement point — one method, reached by HTTP and MCP alike app/tools/catalog.py The seven tools, their schemas, and handlers with no authorization logic app/tools/approvals.py Verifies an approval matches this exact request — six fields app/api/main.py Tasks, approvals, decisions, audit; the proved hop to the agent policy/authz.rego The role matrix, the refund ladder, the reason chain, the resolution See it yourself commands — the two cheap ones, no cluster needed # the graph's safety properties, against the scripted deps python -m pytest app/agent/tests -q # the policy, exactly as CI runs it (OPA is not installed locally) docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/w -w /w openpolicyagent/opa:1.9.0 test policy/ commands — against a running platform # the whole safety story, as a sequence of attacks that must fail ./scripts/attack-tests.sh # one run, end to end, including the interrupt ./scripts/show-trace.sh # the agent's own cases, against whatever model is configured kubectl exec deploy/agent -c agent -- python -m app.agent.evals --live Glossary delegation The verified facts about a request: which user, which roles, which tenant, which workload is acting. The only source of truth a tool call has about \"who\". SPIFFE ID A workload's name, e.g. spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent . Both ends of every internal hop present one. SVID The credential behind that name — X.509 for mTLS, JWT when a token must be passed along. interrupt The graph's pause. State is checkpointed, the run returns to the caller, and Command(resume=…) continues it — the node re-executes, so it must be side-effect free. enforcement point The one method where authority is granted. Everything above it proposes; nothing below it re-decides. require_approval Policy's third answer. Not a denial — a hold, until a person with the manager role approves that exact request. clarification A pause for an argument only a person has, not for a permission. It is bounded (two asks) and the answer is judged by policy like any other argument — answering is not approving. observation What one tool call returned, kept in the run's state and shown to the model on the next step. Only calls that happened produce one, which is why a run cannot loop on a denial. identifier An argument that names something real — customer_id , order_id , ticket_id . The m"},{"p":"index.html","a":"what","t":"Agent Identity Platform","x":"Agent Identity Platform An identity and authorization layer for AI agents . Agents here hold no API keys and no passwords. They prove who they are with a short-lived cryptographically issued identity, act on behalf of a specific person with a scoped token, and every action passes a policy check and, when it matters, a human decision — all of it recorded against the identity that did it. SPIFFE workload identity OAuth 2.0 token exchange OPA policy as code Human approval Audit on identity Kubernetes How to read this document It is written for a developer who has never seen the project: every acronym is defined where it first appears, and there is a glossary at the end. Sections 1–4 are the story; 5–13 are how each part works, with the real code; 14–18 are running, verifying and the project's own history, including its mistakes. Everything quoted here is from the repository itself — file paths are given so you can check."},{"p":"index.html","a":"problem","t":"The problem it removes","x":"The problem it removes An agent that can act on a company's systems needs credentials to those systems. The usual answer is a long-lived secret — an API key in an environment variable, a password in a config file, a service-account JSON on disk. That answer has four problems, and they compound: A secret is a password, not an identity. It says \"someone holds the key\", not who is acting or on whose behalf. It cannot be scoped to one person, so anything the key can do, anyone who steals it can do. It never expires in practice. Rotating it means touching every place it is used, so it stays. A credential that might leak tomorrow is a credential that leaked yesterday. It is copied to wherever the work happens — a pod, a laptop, a CI runner, a log line in a stack trace. Each copy is another way to leak it. Nothing links actions back to a person. When the audit trail says \"a refund was issued\", the useful question — by whom, for whom, and was it allowed — has no answer. This platform replaces the secret with identity : a workload receives a certificate that says what it is, usable only inside the cluster, expiring in an hour, never stored on disk. It acts with a token that names the user it is acting for and the audience it is meant for, valid for minutes. Policy decides what that combination may do, a human decides the risky cases, and the audit trail records the whole chain. The claim, stated so it can be checked There is no static credential between the components of this platform. Not between the agent and the tools, not between the API and Keycloak, not between the services and the database. docs/threat-model.md lists eleven threats and names the test that addresses each; §17 shows how to run them. The eleven threats it is designed against These come from the project's own threat model, and they are the yardstick every design decision is measured against. Threat What it means Answered by T1 Stolen credential impersonates a workload A key copied out of a pod Short-lived SVIDs fetched from the Workload API, never on disk ( §5 ) T2 Token forwarding Passing a token on to something it was not meant for Audience-bound exchange, and a named caller on machine hops ( §6 ) T3 Prompt injection The model being talked into something The model never authorizes; policy does, and the agent only offers permitted tools ( §7 ) T4 Approval bypass Doing the held action another way The tool server refuses the action without a verified approval ( §8 ) T5 Privilege escalation Reaching beyond your role Deny-by-default policy, server-side role checks, separation of duties T6 Secret leakage into git A secret committed Generated secrets, a pre-commit hook, gitleaks in CI over the full history T7 Secret/PII leakage into logs and traces A token or personal data recorded Redaction before anything is written ( §13 ) T8 PII exposure via the model Personal data reaching the provider The gateway is the only thing holding the model credential; PII needs its own role and approval ( §10"},{"p":"index.html","a":"picture","t":"The big picture","x":"The big picture One pod per job, a shared identity system underneath, and two security regimes with a deliberate line between them: our own workloads prove who they are with SPIFFE , and third-party software we did not write is wrapped by a service mesh . The diagram is the whole architecture; the rest of this document explains each box. HOST / OUTSIDE OUR WORKLOADS — SPIFFE mTLS, CALLER NAMED THIRD-PARTY — MESH mTLS browser a person, no SVID ingress (nginx) terminates the TLS https api login · tasks · audit agent LangGraph + approvals tools the enforcement point gateway the only model credential api, and our hops --> https → http (in-cluster) SVID both sides keycloak logins, token exchange OPA the policy decisions postgres approvals, audit, state otel → jaeger traces (+ prometheus) sandbox a fake vendor system SPIRE server + agent issues every SVID in this picture — 1h for X.509, 5m for JWT — and rotates the CA itself workload API The two regimes, and why Our services are our code, so they can fetch a certificate, present it, and check the peer's name. Keycloak, OPA, Postgres and the sandbox are software we did not write: they have no idea what SPIFFE is, and bolting identity into them would be fiction. The mesh encrypts those hops instead. The reasoning is recorded as ADR-0012 . Where the line falls The violet box is encrypted by us and checked by us — both sides present an SVID, and on the machine routes the caller also has to be named . The teal box is encrypted by the mesh , whose identity is the pod's, not the application's. The browser sits outside both: a person cannot hold a certificate, so TLS terminates at an ingress."},{"p":"index.html","a":"journey","t":"One request, end to end","x":"One request, end to end Nothing explains a system like following a single action through it. This is the demo's central scenario: a support agent is asked to refund an order. Each step shows the real request and the real response, trimmed to the interesting fields. person api agent gateway keycloak spire tools opa sandbox IDENTITY PLAN ACT APPROVAL RESULT 1. sign in 2. password grant 3. user token (roles, tenant) 4. POST /tasks 5. run(task, token) 6. allowed tools? 7. plan — which tool? 8. → provider (the key lives here) 9. {tool, args} 10. JWT-SVID (aud=tools) 11. exchange → tool token 12. call tool (scoped token) 13. decide 14. require_approval 15. 428 — held 16. create approval 17. manager approves 18. resume 19. call again (approval_id) 20. do the work 21. result 22. result 23. outcome 24. the result One run, in the order it actually happens. Notice what is not in the trust-flow order: the model is consulted first (7–9) to choose the tool, and the token exchange (11) happens only once a tool is chosen. The colour follows the trust-flow diagram — blue is identity and delegation, green is the tools and policy, violet is the model boundary, red is a held action. The run can loop (S19): after step 22 the agent may plan again with the result. Every stage writes an audit event to the API. Prefer to walk it in 3D? One run, in time → the same 24 steps as an interactive 3D scene . A person signs in. The browser posts credentials to the API, which exchanges them at Keycloak and returns a token the UI keeps in sessionStorage for the tab's lifetime. Note what comes back with it — the roles the platform will act on, and the tools those roles permit, resolved from policy: POST /auth/login → the session { \"user\": \"alice\", \"roles\": [\"support_rep\"], \"tenant\": \"acme\", \"tools\": [\"crm.customer.read\",\"crm.orders.list\",\"refunds.issue\", \"refunds.quote\",\"tickets.read\",\"tickets.reply.draft\"], \"expires_at\": 1789954090, // the access token: 300 seconds (the realm's lifespan) \"refresh_token\": \"…\", // renews it silently; rotated on every use \"refresh_expires_at\": 1789955890 // the session's real limit (Q19) } The access token is a bearer credential and lives five minutes — a security property (revocation counts within one lifetime), not a session length. The session does not end with it: the UI renews the token in the background from the refresh token, so an active tab is not sent back to sign-in every five minutes. It still ends on two clocks, both Keycloak's: an idle window of 30 minutes that slides on each renewal, and an absolute 10-hour ceiling from sign-in. When either passes (or the session is revoked), the UI returns you to sign-in rather than showing you empty tables ( §18 explains why that sentence is in a document like this). The task is sent. The browser calls POST /tasks with the token and a sentence: \"Issue a refund of 200 dollars for order o-1001\" . The API does not do the work — it forwards the task to the agent, and that hop is a SPIFFE hop: the API pres"},{"p":"index.html","a":"identity","t":"Workload identity","x":"Workload identity The foundation is a standard called SPIFFE (Secure Production Identity Framework for Everyone) and an implementation of it called SPIRE . The idea is simple: a piece of software running in your infrastructure should be able to prove what it is without being given a secret to prove it with. The words, defined once Term What it actually is Workload Any program with an identity — here, a pod's main container. The agent, the API, the tool server are workloads. Trust domain The namespace identities are issued in. This platform's is acme.com , and it is documentation, not a secret. SPIFFE ID A URI naming one workload: spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent . Read it right to left: service account agent , in namespace agent-platform , in trust domain acme.com . SVID The credential behind that ID. Two forms are used here: an X.509-SVID (a certificate, for TLS) and a JWT-SVID (a short signed token, for saying who is calling in a header). Workload API A Unix socket inside the pod. A workload asks it for its identity; it is not handed a password to use with it. Attestation How SPIRE decides a node and a pod are genuine before issuing anything — here, the Kubernetes projected service-account token, checked with the API server. There is one agent, and the platform is built for exactly one One agent workload, one SPIFFE ID — spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent — held by two replicas that are interchangeable: a run's state is checkpointed in Postgres, so either replica can resume it, and many runs run at once, all as the same agent acting for different people. Policy trusts exactly that ID ( is_trusted if input.agent == trusted_agent in policy/authz.rego ), so a second agent would be refused as an untrusted workload until it is registered and the policy names it. Everything else is already keyed per agent — the gateway's rate and cost limits, the audit's spiffe_id — which is what would make that a policy and manifest change rather than a redesign. It has not been written down until now; see the agent, end to end for the flow itself. How a pod gets an identity The SPIRE agent runs on each node (a DaemonSet) and proves to the SPIRE server that it is a real agent on a real node, by presenting a Kubernetes service-account token the server checks with the API server. That is k8s_psat attestation. The server decides what that node's agent may vouch for, from registration entries : \"a pod in namespace agent-platform using service account agent may be issued spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent \". scripts/setup.sh creates one entry per attested agent — because with more than one node, each node has its own agent, and an entry parented to a single agent leaves pods on every other node with \"no identity issued\" . The pod's container asks the Workload API socket for its identity. It never presents a password to obtain it; the node's attestation is the root. What comes back expires — an hour for a certificate, five minutes for the J"},{"p":"index.html","a":"delegation","t":"Delegation, explained","x":"Delegation, explained Identity answers \"who are you?\". Delegation answers \"who are you acting for , and what were you allowed to do?\" The mechanism is OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange ( RFC 8693 ): a token is traded for a different token. Why trade at all? Because a token that works everywhere is a token worth stealing. The platform's rule is that every token names an audience — the service it is for — and only that service accepts it. sdk/agentnhi/tokens.py — three checks, in order 1. **Signature and issuer** — it really came from our authorization server. 2. **Audience (`aud`)** — it was minted for *this* service. This is what makes a token stolen from one hop useless on the next. 3. **Issued-to (`azp`)** — it was issued to the workload we expect. Defense in depth: even with a matching audience, a token issued to someone else is refused. The same delegation, traced (browser path) browser ──token(aud=api)──▶ api │ exchanges at Keycloak: \"give me a token for mcp-tools, │ for this user, issued to me (the agent)\" ▼ token(aud=mcp-tools, sub=alice, tenant=acme, roles=[support_rep], azp=portal, act={sub: spiffe://…/sa/agent}) │ ▼ tools ← verifies signature, aud, azp, roles… then policy… then approval The two questions every hop answers A well-designed system asks both, and this one does, from different mechanisms: Question Answered by Failure looks like Is the transport peer genuine? (a real workload, not something that copied a token) The SVID on both ends of the TLS connection ( §11 ) The handshake never completes — there is no application-level \"please authenticate\" step to attack Is it allowed to do this? Token claims ( aud , azp , roles, tenant) plus policy, plus a verified approval where required A refusal naming the reason: your role may not call refunds.issue And on machine hops, a name A certificate proves a workload holds an SVID issued by the right authority; it does not say which workload. So the hops between the API, agent and tool server carry the caller's SPIFFE ID in a JWT-SVID, checked against SPIRE's public keys and an allow-list: app/api/authz.py — the dependency that names a machine caller def require_workload(*allowed: str): \"\"\"Dependency factory: a **machine** caller must be one of these workloads. For the routes a workload calls rather than a browser (creating an approval, verifying one, ingesting audit). The name is the caller's JWT-SVID, verified against SPIRE's JWKS — the same check the gateway makes, shared via `app/common/workload.py`. \"\"\" Why that mattered Before this, POST /audit/events took no credential at all: any pod inside the mesh could write an audit record. The handshake was encrypted, which felt like enough, and said nothing about who was on the other end. Now only the agent and the tool server may ingest audit, and they have to prove it. Everything expires, on purpose Credential Lifetime Consequence Your sign-in token 300 s The UI revalidates the session and asks you to sign in again JWT-SVID (a machine hop) 5 min A mac"},{"p":"index.html","a":"policy","t":"Policy as code","x":"Policy as code Authorization is not scattered through the services as if user.is_admin . It is a policy written in Rego and evaluated by OPA (Open Policy Agent), so that the rules can be read, tested and versioned on their own ( policy/authz.rego , ADR-0003). Deny by default, three outcomes Every tool call resolves to exactly one of: allow , deny , or require approval . Nothing needs a rule to be forbidden. The matrix, and who may call what The heart of the policy is a role → tool table. This is the demo's, verbatim from policy/authz.rego : Role Tools it may call support_rep crm.customer.read, crm.orders.list, tickets.read, tickets.reply.draft, refunds.quote, refunds.issue billing crm.orders.list, refunds.quote, refunds.issue read_only crm.customer.read, crm.orders.list, tickets.read privacy privacy.pii.read, crm.customer.read, crm.orders.list, tickets.read manager crm.customer.read, crm.orders.list, tickets.read, refunds.quote (and approving — not a tool) platform_admin — deliberately none: it administers users, and administers nothing else Two further rules make it more than a table. A tool can require approval rather than permission, and a tenant can replace a role's row with its own (slice S8) — so \"billing\" at one customer need not mean what it means at another. The API asks for the caller's tenant when it asks what is permitted, which is what keeps the offered set equal to the allowed set. Rules that are numbers policy/authz.rego — the refund rules, in full auto_refund_limit := 50 # auto): needs approval; above: denied This is the mechanism the whole demo turns on. A $25 refund is allowed. A $200 refund is held for a manager. A $1000 refund is refused outright. The agent cannot talk its way past any of it: it does not evaluate the policy, it asks it — and the tool server asks again on the way through. Where it is enforced — one choke point Both transports (HTTP and MCP) pass through the same function, which is why a second transport cannot be a way around the first: app/tools/enforcement.py — the enforcement point 1. **Verifies the token** — signature, issuer, audience, and the workload it was issued to. 2. **Asks OPA** — allow / deny / require-approval, deny by default. 3. **Requires a real approval** when policy demands one — confirmed by the approvals service, not asserted by the caller. \"The LLM proposes, policy disposes\" The model is only ever offered the tools the caller's roles permit, so prompt-injection has nothing to aim at — and even if the model asked for something else, the tool server evaluates policy itself. The offer is a courtesy; the check is the control. Versions and signatures Every decision records policy_version — the revision the bundle was built from — so an old decision can be read next to the rule that produced it ( docs/policy-lifecycle.md ). The bundle is a build artifact: stamped by scripts/build-bundle.sh , signed with cosign, and mounted into OPA. Because the files are mounted with subPath (which does not upda"},{"p":"index.html","a":"approval","t":"The human in the loop","x":"The human in the loop Policy can say \"not without a person\". That is the interesting case, because it is where a system most often cheats: it asks a UI for confirmation and treats the answer as authority. Here the approval is a fact recorded in the database , and the tool server checks the fact itself. The shape of a held action The policy decision is require approval . The agent does not proceed and does not silently substitute another tool. It stops, with a reason written for a person to read: \"requires manager approval: refund of 200 is above the auto-approval limit of 50\" . The run is checkpointed. The agent is a state machine (LangGraph, ADR-0004) whose state is persisted in Postgres — so the pause survives a restart of the agent itself, and resuming is not a matter of hoping the process is still alive. An approval record is created by the agent calling the API over a SPIFFE hop, with its own identity attached. The record names the tool, the exact arguments, the requesting user, and the agent's SPIFFE ID. A person decides — in the Approvals tab, or by API — optionally adding a note that becomes part of the record. The demo enforces separation of duties : you cannot approve your own request, and the platform_admin role cannot approve anything, because administering users is a different job from deciding business actions. The run resumes, and the tool server verifies. Not \"the caller says it was approved\" — the tool server asks the approvals service whether an approval exists for precisely this tool, these arguments, this user and this agent. A copy of a legitimate approval for something else is useless (threat T4). Why \"verify, don't trust\" is the whole design The agent could lie; it is software, and software with a model in it can be talked into things. So no component downstream of the model is asked to believe anything the model influenced. The approval is checked against the database of approvals, and the identity of the checker is checked by the certificate it presented to get there. What an approver sees The UI shows each held action as a card: the tool, the arguments, the delegation chain (agent → user → tool), the reason policy gave, and a decision form with an optional note. Once decided, the card states the outcome prominently — approved in green, denied in red — for everyone looking at the run, including the approver who decided it. One detail from the project's own history: that denial message used to be grey prose, rendered only for people who were not approvers — so the one person who could not see the denial was the manager who had just denied it, who fell through to a live Approve/Deny form and could decide the same run twice. §18 . The same gate for privacy Reading a customer's personal data is a read , and a read cannot be undone. The privacy.pii.read tool therefore requires the privacy role and an approval, and every attempt — allowed or not — is written to the audit trail with the reason. docs/privacy.md walks the whole pa"},{"p":"index.html","a":"tools","t":"The tool boundary","x":"The tool boundary Every action an agent can take ends at one service. The tool server is where \"the LLM proposes, policy disposes\" is actually enforced ( app/tools/ ) — and the only place it needs to be. One implementation, two transports Transport Used by Why HTTP The agent's direct tool calls Simple, inspectable, and easy to call from a test MCP (Model Context Protocol) The agent, when it is given tools as an MCP server An open standard for exposing tools to models (ADR-0005) — adding it must not add a second way around policy Both funnel through the same function, ToolEnforcer.call(...) , which is the single place that verifies the token, names the calling workload, asks policy, and requires a verified approval. One choke point is why the MCP path cannot be a loophole. The tools themselves are deliberately dull Seven tools: read a customer, list orders, issue or quote a refund, read a ticket, draft a reply, and read personal data. They live in app/tools/catalog.py and call into app/simulators/ — synthetic CRM, orders, payments and ticketing data, which is all this platform ever touches (ADR-0006). The sandbox stands in for a vendor A real integration is a system you do not control, which may be slow, unavailable, or stateful in ways you have to remember. The sandbox is a small service with its own database and its own volume, and the tools reach it through a seam ( simulator | http ) that a real backend can replace without touching the rest of the platform ( docs/integrations.md , slice S9). Refusals, denials and errors are different things A denial is policy saying no. A refusal is the agent reporting that it could not act — and it says why : an unreachable model, an unparseable reply and a disallowed tool are three different messages, not one. A tool error is neither: asking to refund an order that does not exist answers unknown order , because the platform was allowed to try and the vendor said no (slice S13) — which the UI reports as allowed, but nothing happened , since \"allowed\" is what policy said and not what the tool did. And a question is none of these: since S18, a task that is missing an argument the tool requires pauses and asks for it, rather than ending (see the agent, end to end ). Why this distinction earns its own slice \"The agent didn't do it\" is the least useful sentence an agent platform can produce. Every one of those cases has a different fix — a policy change, a network change, a prompt change — and telling them apart is the difference between a system you can operate and one you have to guess about."},{"p":"index.html","a":"gateway","t":"The LLM gateway","x":"The LLM gateway The agent needs a model. The model provider needs a credential. If the agent held that credential, a prompt-injected agent — or a curious engineer with kubectl exec — could spend the company's model budget, and the key would have to be rotated wherever the agent runs. So the credential lives in exactly one service, and the agent is only allowed to ask that service for text (ADR-0009, docs/llm-gateway.md ). How the gateway decides who is asking The gateway listens only over mTLS with an SVID — there is no plain listener at all. A caller must complete a handshake with a certificate from the trust domain, so \"who is calling\" is never a matter of opinion. On top of that, the caller presents a JWT-SVID for the gateway's audience, verified against SPIRE's public keys. That is what makes the accounting exact: limits are keyed on the caller's SPIFFE ID , not on an API key shared by everything that can reach it. Rate and cost limits are applied per agent — a rate per minute and a token budget per day — and they are stored in Postgres , so a restart of the gateway does not reset them to zero. Swapping the model The model is named in exactly one place — a ConfigMap the gateway reads at startup, rendered from the gitignored .env — so changing it touches no manifest and rebuilds no image: one command, local models ./scripts/use-model.sh qwen3:30b-a3b # swap ./scripts/use-model.sh llama3.2:3b # and back ./scripts/use-model.sh --eval qwen3:30b-a3b # swap, then score it with the live evals Which model you choose is not cosmetic. Measured with the eval suite on one machine: a 30B MoE scored 6/8 , a 3B scored 4–5/8 — and the 3B's failures include \"PII is refused for a support rep\" , a security guardrail rather than a nicety. The portable default in the repository is qwen3:30b-a3b ; llama3.2:3b is a quarter of the size and much quicker. Two things the first swap taught us A thinking model (Qwen3 and friends) puts its JSON in thinking and returns response empty, so the agent reported an unparseable reply while the model had answered correctly — the gateway now asks for think: false and falls back, which took that model from 1/8 to 6/8. And the audit had been recording the model the caller asked for, which is usually nothing: it now records the model resolved for the call, which is what makes \"which model decided this?\" answerable. Provider-agnostic by construction The gateway speaks an OpenAI-compatible API and a local Ollama endpoint. The default is local: ./start.sh needs no model key at all, which is why the platform runs end to end on a laptop with nothing in the cloud. If you set a provider key, it is consumed by the gateway and by nothing else. Hosted providers were finished in S17 . The agent deliberately names no model — it asks for an answer, not a vendor — so the gateway now puts the model it resolved into the forwarded request; a provider used to receive none and fail. And because a strict provider may reject the formal JSON hint, LLM_STRI"},{"p":"index.html","a":"transport","t":"Transport security","x":"Transport security Every hop is encrypted, but not by the same thing — and the line between the two mechanisms is deliberate, recorded in ADR-0012 . Getting this wrong is easy in a way that looks right: a service mesh encrypts everything, so it is tempting to leave it at that. The catch is that the mesh proves the identity of the mesh . The application never learns which workload is on the other end, and a hop can be encrypted while a pod that copied a token walks straight through it. Layer Covers The identity it proves SPIFFE mTLS (ours) Every hop between workloads we wrote: agent ⇄ gateway , agent ⇄ tools , agent ⇄ api The workloads' own SVIDs — and on machine routes, the caller's name Service mesh (Linkerd) Everything else: Keycloak, OPA, Postgres, the sandbox, the observability stack, and the /metrics scrape The mesh's workload identity — good encryption, no name the application can use TLS at the ingress The browser's hop to the API A certificate from a CA this project generates, with the hostname the browser types in it Why the third-party hops stay on the mesh Keycloak, OPA and Postgres are products we did not write: they do not speak SPIFFE and cannot present an SVID. Requiring one would be fiction. The sandbox stands in for a vendor's system, and a real vendor has no SVID either — so putting it with the third-party software models the right thing. For those, the mesh is the honest answer: no TLS code in the products, no certificates of ours to distribute, and it covers the hops you would forget, including telemetry. One mechanical detail that matters Our SPIFFE ports skip the mesh proxy The pods carry config.linkerd.io/skip-inbound-ports: \"8443\" (and the outbound equivalent). Without it, Linkerd terminates the connection — proving its identity and hiding the peer's SVID from our server, so the application's check has nothing to check. The fix is this project's own lesson, applied to itself, and tls-check.sh asserts the consequence: a client with no SVID cannot complete a handshake on a hop we own. The alternative was measured and rejected The obvious way to keep the two identity systems from overlapping is to take our pods out of the mesh. Before doing that, the project measured it: with the API pod uninjected, a login still worked against a meshed Keycloak — in plaintext. A client without a proxy loses encryption on all of its outbound hops, not just the ones being retired. That measurement is why the pods stay injected and only the port is skipped. How this is verified, rather than asserted ./scripts/tls-check.sh reports three things, each from where it can actually be seen: ./scripts/tls-check.sh MESH EDGES (SECURED = mTLS) every edge the mesh carries must be √ HOPS WE OWN (SPIFFE, caller named) asked from inside a pod that holds an SVID: agent → tools mTLS required ✓ ✓ refused unnamed; admitted named (403) agent → api mTLS required ✓ ✓ refused unnamed; admitted named (200) BROWSER EDGE (TLS at the ingress) certificate chains to our C"},{"p":"index.html","a":"tenancy","t":"Multi-tenancy","x":"Multi-tenancy Two customers use this platform. Neither may see the other's anything. The design decision that makes that enforceable is small and easy to miss: the tenant is part of the caller's identity, not a parameter they pass . It comes from the token A user's tenant is set when their account exists and travels in the token they present. There is no ?tenant= anywhere — which is what stops the oldest trick in the book, asking for someone else's data politely. It is enforced everywhere it matters Policy resolves a role's tools for that tenant (a tenant may replace a role's row entirely). Approvals are stored and queried per tenant. The audit trail is scoped to the caller's tenant. Every data accessor checks it. The demo signs in grace — a support representative at a second tenant, globex — and ./scripts/tenancy-tests.sh walks the layers one at a time: the claim, the policy, the approval store, and the data itself: ./scripts/tenancy-tests.sh tenant from the token… policy resolves globex's own matrix… an approval made in acme is invisible in globex… and the same customer id reads differently in each tenant. A second tenant's data is unreachable from the first. Threat T9, and the reason the platform is \"multi-tenant\" rather than \"multi-user with good intentions\"."},{"p":"index.html","a":"data","t":"Data and durability","x":"Data and durability Nothing in this platform is stateful in memory, and that is a design position rather than an accident: a service can only be replicated — and therefore survive a node dying — if its state lives somewhere that outlives it ( docs/ha.md ). What Where it lives What it survives Approvals, and their decisions and notes Postgres, tenant-scoped A restart of the API, the agent, or the database itself Agent run checkpoints Postgres The agent restarting mid-run — a held action resumes where it stopped The audit trail Postgres, redacted on the way in Anything, including a full re-run of the setup script SPIRE's registry (which pod may be which identity) Postgres, in its own spire role A SPIRE restart: no workload loses its identity SPIRE's signing keys A PersistentVolume A SPIRE restart keeping the same CA — so no SVID anywhere needs reissuing Keycloak's realm and accounts Postgres, its own keycloak role Restarts, and re-running setup: an account enrolled today exists tomorrow The sandbox's synthetic vendor data Its own volume The pod being rescheduled (slice S9) Model rate and token budgets Postgres A gateway restart — otherwise a deploy would be a way to reset your limits Redaction happens before anything is written Audit events are the one place where \"we logged everything\" becomes a liability: logs outlive the data they describe and travel to places with weaker access control. The platform redacts tokens and personal data before the record is stored, so the trail can name what happened and who did it without carrying the secret or the person's data with it (threat T7). The Audit tab states this on the page, because a reader deserves to know what they are looking at. The honest limits of the demo's storage One Postgres instance, on a volume that is node-local (kind's local-path provisioner) — so it cannot follow its pod to another node, and deleting the cluster deletes it. That is fine for a demonstration and wrong for production, which is why the Helm chart takes a database.url pointing at a managed, replicated database. The distinction is documented rather than glossed: docs/data-stores.md ."},{"p":"index.html","a":"deploy","t":"Deployment","x":"Deployment The whole platform runs on a laptop. ./start.sh creates a Kubernetes cluster (kind), builds the images, deploys everything, and prints a URL; the first run takes ten to fifteen minutes, and every run after it resumes in seconds. That is not a toy convenience — it is how the project stays honest, because a system you cannot bring up from scratch is a system whose instructions have quietly rotted. What setup.sh does, and why each step is gated The bring-up is twelve numbered steps, and it refuses to report success until each one has actually been observed to work. Several of those gates exist because the project was burned by not having them: # Step The gate before moving on 1 kind cluster It exists, and the pinned context is reachable 2 Build and load images Every image is stamped with the revision it came from 3 Namespace, mesh, secrets Linkerd is up and the namespace is injected 4 Postgres Ready, with a least-privilege role per consumer 5 SPIRE Agents attested, entries registered, and every replica serving the same trust bundle 6 Keycloak The realm was imported by a job, against the database 7 OPA Serving the policy bundle, and it prints the revision 8 Observability Collector, Jaeger, Prometheus and Grafana ready 9 The services api, tools, agent, gateway and sandbox rolled out 10 The browser edge An https fetch with the generated CA — never -k 11 Autoscaling The metric APIs answer, including the custom ones 12 Identity, live A real pod fetches a real SVID from the Workload API Then it asks the question that catches the most mistakes of all: are the pods running what we just built? check-images.sh compares the revision stamped into each running image against the current commit, and marks a dirty tree as dirty — because \"the image matches HEAD\" is a claim worth checking, and a lie if you edited anything afterwards. Two faces of the same platform kind — the whole thing, locally One control plane and two workers, so replication and spreading are real rather than theoretical. The one host port is the browser edge — a fixed mapping from host 8443 to the ingress controller's NodePort, not a port-forward, so nothing has to stay alive for the URL to keep working. It reaches the ingress , not the API. Helm — the same services anywhere A values-driven chart ( deploy/helm/agent-platform ) for the parts that are yours to configure: replicas, environment, the LLM provider, the Postgres DSN, mesh injection, ingress and TLS, autoscaling, observability. SPIRE, Keycloak, the realm and the signed policy bundle are prerequisites it expects you to provide. The identity tier can be a failover pair SPIRE's registration registry has lived in Postgres since the first hardening slice, but its CA key was a file on a volume — so two SPIRE replicas would each mint their own CA and hand out different trust bundles, which is worse than having one. The KeyManager is now switchable : .env — the switch, and its default SPIRE_KEY_MANAGER=disk # default: no credentials,"},{"p":"index.html","a":"ci","t":"CI/CD and supply chain","x":"CI/CD and supply chain Ten checks run on every pull request, and all ten are required: a red one blocks the merge. They are chosen so that each covers a different way to be wrong. Check What it would catch app-tests The application's behaviour, including the tests born from real failures ( §18 ) sdk-tests The identity and token code the services share opa-tests The policy: 45 Rego tests, including the allow/deny/approval boundaries helm The chart lints and renders, with the toggles on and off web-build The UI still typechecks and compiles repo-checks Shell syntax, the config files, the pre-commit hook, and the self-tests that scripts carry secrets gitleaks over the working tree and the full history sast Static analysis of the source dependencies Known vulnerabilities in what the project pulls in supply-chain Trivy over images and manifests — a misconfiguration or a leaked secret in a file Signing, and deploying behind a human Releases sign the images and the policy bundle with keyless cosign (Sigstore): the signature is tied to the workflow that produced it and recorded in a transparency log, so there is no signing key to store, rotate, or leak (ADR-0010, docs/supply-chain.md ). The deploy workflow then reverses the direction of trust: it verifies those signatures before applying anything, and the apply sits behind a GitHub Environment approval — a human gate on deployment, which is the same idea as the product's own approval gate, one level up. Exceptions are meant to be temporary Trivy's ignore file currently has no entries , and the file says so along with the history: Keycloak once needed an exception for a read-only root filesystem because its development mode wrote into the image at startup. When the platform moved to production mode (slice S4), the cause went away — so the exception did too. A list of exceptions is a list of things you intend to fix."},{"p":"index.html","a":"ops","t":"Operations","x":"Operations Everything an operator does is a script, because a runbook that is not executable rots. These are the ones worth knowing: Command What it is for ./start.sh Bring it up (or resume it) and print the URL ./status.sh Is it up, how many pods are ready, what URL ./stop.sh [--delete] Stop it (keeping data) or remove the cluster entirely ./scripts/setup.sh The gated bring-up; idempotent, and safe to re-run ./scripts/demo.sh The happy path, including the approval ./scripts/use-model.sh Swap the model the gateway uses — no manifest edit, no rebuild ( §10 ) ./scripts/attack-tests.sh Six attacks, each of which must fail ./scripts/tenancy-tests.sh One tenant's data unreachable from another ./scripts/role-tools.sh Who may call which tool — deterministic, no model involved ./scripts/demo-roles.sh Enrolment, a role grant, and enforcement server-side ./scripts/tls-check.sh Every hop authenticated, and by which mechanism ( §11 ) ./scripts/ha-check.sh Replicas spread, and an eviction is survivable ./scripts/check-images.sh Are the running pods the revision you built? ./scripts/show-trace.sh The latest trace, with the identity attributes on its spans ./scripts/build-bundle.sh · sign-bundle.sh · verify-bundle.sh Build, sign and verify the policy artifact ./logs.sh A readable tail across the services Observability: the question the platform exists to answer Traces and metrics are not decoration here. The interesting question is \"which agent, acting for which user, asked for what, and what did policy decide?\" — and that question spans services. Spans carry identity attributes, the API exports platform counters at /metrics , Prometheus scrapes them, and Grafana draws the dashboard: agent_platform_logins_total result agent_platform_audit_events_total event agent_platform_approvals_pending agent_platform_http_requests_total method · route · status The platform counters are derived from the audit stream , deliberately: every service forwards audit events to the API, so one ingest point yields policy decisions, approvals, logins and admin actions. The dashboard and the audit log cannot drift apart, because they are the same data. Scaling and redundancy Service Replicas Scales on api 2 → 5 CPU 70%, the approval backlog (keep it at 5), and per-pod request rate (20/s) tools · agent · gateway · opa 2 → 5 CPU 70% spire-server 1 (or 2 with a shared KeyManager) Not autoscaled — identity is a small, steady load The floor of two is not arbitrary: it matches the HA replica count, so autoscaling can never quietly take you below the redundancy you asked for, and scale-down is slowed so a short burst does not immediately drop capacity. Self-healing, and the limit of a probe Every container carries a liveness probe, not just a readiness one — because a readiness probe is a signal, and only liveness restarts a process. That distinction cost this project a day and a half of a broken bring-up ( §18 ), and it is why the gateway serves a health-only plaintext listener: an mTLS-only"},{"p":"index.html","a":"verified","t":"What is verified, and how","x":"What is verified, and how Every claim in this document is checkable from a checkout of the repository. Verification happens at three levels, and they are useful for different reasons. Level Runs Proves Gates During setup.sh That this deployment, right now, actually works — an https fetch with the real CA, a real pod fetching a real SVID, two SPIRE replicas agreeing on a trust bundle Tests In CI on every change Behaviour, offline and deterministic: application logic, the token and identity code, the policy rules, the chart, the UI build Suites Against a running cluster The things only a live system can answer: who may call which tool, what an attack gets, whether a second tenant is truly unreachable The numbers, as of this writing 215 application tests passed 23 skipped (need a database) 45/45 policy tests 34 SDK tests 10 required CI checks 9 end-to-end suites The skips are the Postgres-backed tests: they need a database, so CI provides one and a local run skips them. To run them locally, start a throwaway Postgres and point the tests at it: running the tests that need a database docker run -d --rm --name ap-test-pg -e POSTGRES_DB=agent_platform \\ -e POSTGRES_USER=agent -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=agent -p 55432:5432 postgres:17-alpine PGHOST=localhost PGPORT=55432 PGUSER=agent PGPASSWORD=agent PGDATABASE=agent_platform \\ .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q Prove it yourself the whole thing, from a checkout ./start.sh # build, deploy, print the URL (10–15 min the first time) ./status.sh # pods ready, and the URL ./scripts/demo.sh # read → refund → approval → issued ./scripts/attack-tests.sh # six attacks, every one of which must fail ./scripts/tenancy-tests.sh # one tenant's data unreachable from another ./scripts/role-tools.sh # who may call which tool (no model involved) ./scripts/demo-roles.sh # enrolment grants nothing; a role is enforced server-side ./scripts/tls-check.sh # every hop authenticated, and by which mechanism ./scripts/ha-check.sh # replicas spread; an eviction is survivable ./scripts/check-images.sh # are the running pods the revision you built? .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q # 215 passed, 23 skipped docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/w -w /w openpolicyagent/opa:1.9.0 test policy/ # 45/45 helm lint deploy/helm/agent-platform What is not verified here, said plainly The browser interactions. There is no headless browser in this environment, so the UI's behaviour is verified by hand — the API calls it makes are all covered, but \"does the card look right when you click Deny\" is a human's job. Several UI fixes in §18 were found exactly this way, by a person looking at the page. The AWS KMS path was exercised once against a real account: two SPIRE replicas sharing one CA, a replica deleted mid-flight with a workload still receiving a fresh SVID and no workload restarting. It is not exercised in CI, because it needs credentials — so treat it as verified-and-not-regression-tested. The mesh is required for the third-party hops' encryption. Without the Linkerd "},{"p":"index.html","a":"built","t":"How this was built","x":"How this was built This section is unusual in product documentation, and it is here deliberately. This project's distinguishing quality is not that it worked the first time — it is that its failures are recorded, explained, and turned into checks . If you are evaluating it, this is the part worth reading: everything below is a claim about how the project behaves when something goes wrong, and every one ends with the test that now catches it. 1 · A process hung for 31 hours and nothing restarted it Jaeger — the trace viewer — stopped responding and stopped logging. The container stayed Running and its readiness probe went on reporting failure, forever. Nothing looked at the failure, because a readiness probe is a signal, not a restart: only a liveness probe tells Kubernetes to do something about it. The bring-up script, which waits for rollouts, could not finish — so the first-run path was broken for a day and a half and only a human could clear it. Now: every container carries a liveness probe, with thresholds chosen per component — about a minute of silence for a stateless service, two minutes for Postgres, where a restart is the most expensive thing Kubernetes can do to it. The gateway also needed code , not a probe: its only listener is mTLS, and a kubelet has no SVID, so it serves /healthz and nothing else on a plaintext port. And the verification taught its own lesson. The first attempt reported \"not restarted\" for four services, which looked like a finding about probes. It was a finding about the test: signals sent to a PID-namespace init process from inside the namespace are discarded — kubectl exec … kill -STOP 1 is a no-op, and the process stays in state S . It has to be signalled from the node, matched by cgroup. Fifteen containers were then hung and watched to recover. 2 · A TLS server served an expired certificate The gateway fetches its identity at startup. Keycloak hands back its cached SVID, which may already be half spent, so a process that restarts on a fixed interval can find itself serving an expired certificate — every handshake fails, and the symptom names the wrong component: \"the model could not be reached\". Now: the restart deadline is derived from the certificate's own notAfter (exit 120 seconds early), and gateway.svid_loaded reports expires_in_seconds at startup so the condition is visible before it bites. 3 · The identity registry lived in a file beside the server SPIRE's registry was a SQLite file, so a restarted server forgot every agent and every entry — and the demo's script deleted Keycloak every run, so accounts did not survive either. Identity was durable in principle and ephemeral in practice. Now: the registry, the realm and the application's own state live in Postgres, each with its own least-privilege role; SPIRE's signing keys are on a volume, so a restart keeps the same CA and no workload needs reissuing. This also inverted the demo's critical path — identity now needs the database — which is exactly why p"},{"p":"index.html","a":"limits","t":"Limits and non-goals","x":"Limits and non-goals Every system is honest only about the boundaries it states. These are the ones this platform names in its own documentation, rather than leaving to be discovered. What it deliberately is not Not an agent framework It is the identity and authorization layer around an agent. The agent is LangGraph, the tool boundary is MCP, the policy engine is OPA — the project's contribution is the layer that makes those three accountable to a person's identity. Not production infrastructure One Postgres, on a node-local volume, is a demonstration choice. SPIRE can run two replicas with a shared KeyManager, but the database remains the shared dependency — which is why the chart takes a managed DSN. Not a claim that one identity system sufficed Two remain, and the boundary between them is the design (ADR-0012). The mesh is load-bearing for the software we did not write. Not real data, ever Everything is synthetic: customers, orders, refunds, personal data. The PCI-aware scoping is real in shape and fictional in content (ADR-0006, docs/data-handling.md ). The residuals, repeated on purpose These are stated in the transport section and in the ADR, and they are repeated here because a limit you have to find is a limit you will miss: Residual Consequence The ingress → api hop is plaintext in-cluster The browser's hop is encrypted; the last leg inside the cluster is not, because the controller carries no sidecar. Meshing the controller closes it. A machine caller's name rides a JWT-SVID, not the certificate Our ASGI server does not expose the peer certificate. The name is audience-bound, five minutes, never logged — but it is a bearer token, not a SAN assertion. The mesh must be present for third-party encryption Without it, those hops are plaintext — and setup says so rather than pretending. Cross-tenant isolation is enforced in the application and the policy Not by separate databases or clusters. For a shared deployment of this shape that is the right trade; for stricter isolation you would go further. The threat model covers eleven threats It does not claim to cover categories outside it — availability under deliberate attack, or the security of the model provider's own systems, are out of scope and named as such."},{"p":"index.html","a":"appendix","t":"Appendix","x":"Appendix Ports, as deployed Service Ports Notes api 8080 · 8443 8080 is the listener for the browser and tooling; 8443 is the SPIFFE listener tools 8000 · 8443 The tool server: 8000 for verification clients, 8443 for the agent agent 8081 · 8443 8443 is SPIFFE; 8081 carries the user→agent path gateway 8443 · 8081 8443 is SPIFFE only ; 8081 serves /healthz and nothing else, for the kubelet sandbox 8090 The simulated vendor system, with its own state on a volume keycloak 8080 · 9000 9000 is the management port the health probes use opa 8181 Policy decisions and its own health postgres 5432 Approvals, checkpoints, audit, SPIRE's registry, Keycloak's realm otel-collector 4317 · 4318 · 8888 · 13133 OTLP gRPC and HTTP, the collector's own metrics, and its health endpoint jaeger 16686 The trace viewer prometheus · grafana 9090 · 3000 Scrape and queries; the provisioned dashboard spire-server · agent 8081 · socket The server's API; workloads use the Unix socket, not a port spire oidc-discovery 11080 Serves the JWKS the gateway and Keycloak use to verify JWT-SVIDs metrics-server · adapter 10250 · 6443 Resource metrics and the custom/external metric APIs ingress-nginx 80 · 443 · 30443 In-cluster; reached from the host through a fixed port mapping, host 8443 → NodePort 30443 Environment variables worth knowing The manifests and the chart are the reference; these are the ones that shape behaviour. Variable Effect SPIFFE_SOCKET · SPIFFE_BUNDLE Where the Workload API socket and the trust bundle are SPIFFE_RESTART_MARGIN_SECONDS How early a service exits before its certificate expires (default 120 s) WORKLOAD_AUDIENCE The audience this service requires of a machine caller's JWT-SVID. Unset means the check is skipped — which the manifests never leave unset TOKEN_AUDIENCE · KC_ISSUER The audience and issuer expected of delegated tokens AGENT_URL · TOOLS_URL · GATEWAY_URL Where each service calls its peers — over the SPIFFE listener OPA_URL · DATABASE_URL / PG* Policy decisions, and durable state (empty means in-memory) LLM_PROVIDER · LLM_BASE_URL · LLM_MODEL · LLM_API_KEY The gateway's provider; the key is consumed only there OLLAMA_URL · OLLAMA_MODEL Which local model, rendered into the gateway's config by setup.sh from .env LLM_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE · LLM_TOKEN_BUDGET_PER_DAY Per-agent limits, keyed on the caller's SPIFFE ID, stored in Postgres LLM_GATEWAY_AUDIENCE · SPIRE_JWKS_URL How the gateway decides which workload is asking SIGNUP_ENABLED Whether the self-service enrollment page is offered SPIRE_KEY_MANAGER (+ SPIRE_KMS_*) Which key manager SPIRE uses, and the AWS settings when it is aws_kms WEB_DIR Where the API finds the built UI to serve Metrics agent_platform_logins_total {result} agent_platform_audit_events_total {event} agent_platform_approvals_pending agent_platform_http_requests_total {method,route,status} exposed for the HPA: approvals_pending · http_requests_per_second The decisions, as records ADR Decision 0001 SPIFFE/SPIRE for workload identity"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"how","t":"Questions, answered","x":"A growing log Questions, answered Every question I asked about this platform while learning it, with the answer the code and the running cluster give — not the answer a diagram suggests. New questions get added as they come up, and each answer says where to look, so you can check it rather than take it on faith. How each answer is built If it is a fact about a file, the file is named. If it is a fact about behaviour, it was seen running — and where it was surprising, the answer says what surprised me. Three pages sit together: the overview explains the whole platform, the agent page follows one task through the code, and this page answers questions — including the ones the other two now had to change for."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"what","t":"What is this platform, in a minute?","x":"01 What is this platform, in a minute? A control plane for AI agents. It answers one question, over and over, per action: \"may this agent, acting for this person, in this tenant, do this — right now?\" — and it answers it in code that does not trust the model. The shape is four of our own services, one identity system underneath, and one place where a model is allowed to live: Piece Its job It may api Login, enrollment, approvals, audit, and the web app Talk to Keycloak; hold the admin client; proxy tasks to the agent agent The graph: plan → call a tool → ask → or pause for a person Propose actions. Hold no model credential tools The enforcement point — the only place authority is granted Call the downstream systems, after policy and any approval gateway The only thing with the model provider's key Reach the model; count and cap spend per calling agent Underneath: SPIRE gives every workload a name and a short-lived credential; Keycloak holds the people and issues their tokens; OPA decides per call; and Postgres keeps the things that must survive a restart — approvals, the audit trail, and paused runs. The phrase to keep in mind is the one the pages repeat: the model proposes, the platform disposes ."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"users","t":"Where are the users stored?","x":"02 Where are the users stored? In Keycloak, in the realm agent-platform . The application keeps no user table of its own — it verifies a token and reads identity out of it. Layer Where Definition (in git) deploy/kind/manifests/keycloak/ (the realm template): users, realm roles, clients Rendered scripts/render.py fills the client secrets from the gitignored .env Imported A one-shot keycloak-import Job, run before the server starts, with --override=false — a no-op once the realm exists Stored at runtime Keycloak's own database in Postgres — not the app's Administered Keycloak's Admin API, via the least-privilege platform-admin client ( manage-users + read-roles, never the bootstrap admin) Inside a token, the role is a realm role and the tenant is a user attribute — which is why the agent's decisions can differ for the same role name in two tenants. The demo users are: User Tenant Role alice acme support_rep manager acme manager admin acme platform_admin priya acme privacy bella acme billing dana acme read_only grace globex support_rep less power grace is the tenancy lesson: the same role name as alice, fewer tools, because the policy gives globex 's support reps its own entry that replaces the default. Enrollment ( POST /enroll ) grants nothing — an admin has to assign the realm role, which is why the demo's carol can sign in and do nothing."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"keycloak-ui","t":"How do I show someone the users really exist?","x":"03 How do I show someone the users really exist? Ask Keycloak, not the app. One command prints the users straight from Keycloak's Admin API; the console is two steps away if you want to click instead. commands — one command, no setup, no admin password ./scripts/show-keycloak-users.sh It runs inside the API pod, which is the whole trick: in there keycloak resolves by service name, so there is no port-forward, no /etc/hosts entry and no browser — and it reuses the API's own least-privilege client, so no master admin credential is involved. Besides the seeded demo users it lists the accounts the suites write: norole , survivor , and two carol-* — the role demo enrolls a fresh one each run and prunes the older ones, so the realm holds two. For the console itself commands — a hosts entry and a port-forward, then a browser echo \"127.0.0.1 keycloak\" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts # once kubectl --context kind-agent-platform -n agent-platform port-forward svc/keycloak 8080:8080 Then http://keycloak:8080/admin , sign in as admin / admin (demo-only, and only reachable this way — the ingress routes / to the API, so no admin console is exposed at the edge), switch the realm to agent-platform , and open Users . Per user, Role mapping is the role and Attributes is the tenant. Two things that puzzle people otherwise Browsing localhost:8080 does not work: the realm pins the console's hostname, so the browser has to resolve the name keycloak — hence the hosts entry. And the realm holds more users than the demo, because the suites write to it: the realm is a live database, not a fixture."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"demo-data","t":"What data does the demo hold?","x":"04 What data does the demo hold? Five orders, three tickets, and the people above — synthetic, served by a sandbox service that keeps its own state. Nothing here is real, and the state survives restarts (it is snapshotted to its own volume). The orders, and which o- numbers exist Order Tenant Customer Total Status Already refunded Refundable o-1001 acme c-100 $50.00 delivered $7,578.00 $0.00 o-1002 acme c-100 $320.00 delivered $200.00 $120.00 o-2001 acme c-200 $25.00 shipped $0.00 $25.00 o-3001 acme c-300 $900.00 delivered $200.00 $700.00 o-9001 globex c-900 $100.00 delivered $0.00 $100.00 Three things that surprise people · o-9001 belongs to globex : asking as acme answers unknown order — tenancy working, not a bug. · o-1001 's refunded figure is enormous because every demo and attack run has refunded it; its refundable is now zero. · the simulator does not enforce refundable > 0 , so a refund against o-1001 still \"issues\". The platform's real limits are the policy ones — ≤ $50 allowed, ≤ $500 with a manager, above that denied. For a clean demo use o-2001 (untouched) or o-1002 . The tickets are where the interesting multi-step task lives: Ticket Tenant Customer Subject t-5001 acme c-100 \"My order arrived damaged\" — a GADGET-B, which is order o-1002 t-5002 acme c-200 \"Where is my order?\" t-9001 globex c-900 the other tenant's So \"refund the order from ticket t-5001\" is a genuine three-step task — read the ticket, list the customer's orders, then refund — which is exactly what the agent's step budget was built for. Customers c-100 , c-200 and c-300 exist for acme; c-900 for globex. commands — ask the running sandbox, which owns the live state kubectl --context kind-agent-platform -n agent-platform exec -i deploy/tools -c tools -- python -c \" import httpx for oid in ('o-1001','o-1002','o-2001','o-3001'): print(oid, httpx.get(f'http://sandbox:8090/orders/{oid}/refund-quote', headers={'X-Tenant':'acme'}).json()) \""},{"p":"questions.html","a":"one-agent","t":"Is there one agent, or many?","x":"05 Is there one agent, or many? One — and the platform is built for exactly one. One workload, one SPIFFE ID, held by two interchangeable replicas. Many runs happen at once, all as the same agent acting for different people. The identity is spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent , and the word workload is doing the work in that sentence: identity is issued per workload, not per replica, which is why both pods prove the same name. Registration is a rule about when an identity may be issued, binding a Kubernetes service account to a name: scripts/setup.sh ensure_entry() { # ensure_entry <service-account> <spiffe-id> local sa=\"$1\" id=\"$2\" entry parent # Drop any existing entries for this identity (e.g. parented to a node that is # gone), then register one per attested agent. for entry in $($SPIRE entry show -socketPath \"$SOCKET\" -spiffeID \"$id\" 2>/dev/null \\ | awk '/Entry ID/{print $NF}'); do $SPIRE entry delete -socketPath \"$SOCKET\" -entryID \"$entry\" >/dev/null done for parent in $($SPIRE agent list -socketPath \"$SOCKET\" 2>/dev/null \\ | awk '/SPIFFE ID/{print $NF}'); do $SPIRE entry create -socketPath \"$SOCKET\" \\ -spiffeID \"$id\" -parentID \"$parent\" \\ -selector \"k8s:ns:$NS\" -selector \"k8s:sa:$sa\" >/dev/null done ok \"registration entry: $id (k8s:sa=$sa)\" } Four workloads are registered that way: agent , gateway , api and tools . And policy then trusts one actor: policy/authz.rego trusted_agent := \"spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent\" A second agent would be refused as denied: untrusted workload identity until it is registered and the policy names it. That is not an accident of configuration — it is the current design, and question 10 is what changing it would take."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"agent-now","t":"What can the agent do now that it couldn't before?","x":"06 What can the agent do now that it couldn't before? Four things, and each one came from someone using it and finding it wanting. None of them changed what is permitted — they changed what the agent does with a thin task, and how honest the record is. Ask instead of refusing S18 \"Refund $200\" with no order named used to end as a refusal. It now pauses and asks which order , because the person has the answer. Bounded at two asks; a tool the role may not call is still refused outright. Take more than one step S19 \"Refund the order from ticket t-5001\" needs the ticket read first. The agent can now go round again with what the call returned — at most three steps, and only when it says it needs another. Every step is judged by policy on its own. Pass identifiers along, never invent them S20 An identifier is kept only if the model has seen it — in the task or in an earlier tool result. Otherwise it is dropped, and the task's own value wins over the model's guess (see question 7 ). \"Allowed\" stops meaning \"it happened\" S21 When the tool answers that it could not do the work, the screen says allowed — but nothing happened , and the tool's answer is recorded in the audit (see question 8 ). The pattern in all four The model proposes; the platform disposes. S18–S20 make the agent better at conversing without giving it a gram more authority, and S21 makes the platform more honest about what happened. If one of these ever read as \"the model got a new power\", the design would have gone wrong — it is worth checking that when reading them."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"approval","t":"When something needs an approval, how does that work?","x":"07 When something needs an approval, how does that work? Yes — there is a real queue, and it is a Postgres table. A request that policy judges too risky is written down, the run pauses , and it stays paused until a different person decides. The queue is what the manager's Approvals tab reads, and its depth is what scales the API. The path of one held action Policy says so. The tool server asks OPA, which answers require_approval for this caller, tool and arguments — e.g. a refund above $50 and no more than $500 — and returns 428 to the agent. Nothing has been executed. The agent writes the request down. It calls the API to create an approval, over a SPIFFE hop, and only the agent's workload identity is allowed to do that — creating an approval is the step that makes a high-risk action possible. The run pauses. The graph's await_decision node raises an interrupt , the run is checkpointed in Postgres, and the requester gets the approval id. The run can be resumed by any replica, and it survives a restart. A person decides. A manager (the Keycloak role) sees it in the queue and approves or denies, optionally with a note. The requester cannot decide their own request. The run resumes, and is judged again. The requester resumes it; the agent calls the tool with the approval id; the tool server verifies that the approval matches this exact request — six fields — and only then executes. The queue itself One row per request: the tool, its arguments as canonical JSON, the person it is for, the agent that asked, the reason, the tenant, the status, who decided it and when. app/approvals/store.py — the table CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS approvals ( id text PRIMARY KEY, tool text NOT NULL, args jsonb NOT NULL, \"user\" text NOT NULL, agent text NOT NULL, reason text NOT NULL, tenant text NOT NULL DEFAULT '', status text NOT NULL, approver text, note text, created_at double precision NOT NULL, decided_at double precision ); There are two backends behind one interface: Postgres when a database is configured (durable, shared by all replicas, and what the demo runs), and in-memory for tests and single-process runs. The queue a person sees is their tenant's pending rows; the number the autoscaler sees counts every tenant , because queue depth is a property of the platform, not of one tenant or one replica. Three things that make it more than a to-do list · Bound to the exact request. Verification compares the approval id, the tool, its arguments, the person, the agent and the tenant — so an approval for a $60 refund cannot be replayed for $600, or for someone else. · Separation of duties, in SQL. The decision is a single statement whose WHERE clause requires the row to still be pending, to be in the approver's tenant, and to not belong to them. Two managers clicking at once cannot both win, and nobody can approve their own request — enforced by the database, not by the UI. · An approval is not authorization. Policy runs again on the resumed call, and the tool server v"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"why-ask","t":"Why didn't it ask for the order?","x":"08 Why didn't it ask for the order? Because the model supplied one — it answered with the field's own name as the value. The agent asks when a required argument is missing ; a guess is not missing, so nothing asked, and a refund against an order literally called order_id went to a manager for approval. Reproduced from a real run. The task was \"Issue a refund of 200 dollars for order \" — nothing after \"order\" — and the approval it created carried: illustrative — the arguments the approval was bound to {\"amount\": 200.0, \"order_id\": \"order_id\"} Four steps, each behaving exactly as designed, which was the problem: The model replied {\"tool\": \"refunds.issue\", \"args\": {\"order_id\": \"order_id\", \"amount\": 200}} . The gap-filling code looked for an order in the task text, found none, and left the model's value alone. The guardrail that exists to catch missing arguments only checked that a required value was present and non-empty — and \"order_id\" is a non-empty string. Policy saw a well-formed $200 refund and held it for a manager. The fix is a rule, not a patch: the model may pass along an identifier it has seen — in the task or in a tool result — and may never invent one. What counts as an identifier is the _id suffix, so amounts and free text are untouched: app/agent/guardrails.py def identifier_args(tool: Tool) -> list[str]: \"\"\"The tool's required arguments that name something that exists in the world. The convention is the `_id` suffix — `customer_id`, `order_id`, `ticket_id`. These are facts the model cannot reason its way to; it either saw one or it is guessing, which is the distinction `resolve_identifiers` enforces. \"\"\" required = (tool.input_schema or {}).get(\"required\") or [] return [name for name in required if name.endswith(\"_id\")] And the fix had a hole that only a retry found The first version searched the task text for the model's value — and retrying the same task then got through as {\"order_id\": \"order\"} , because the word \"order\" appears in \"for order \" . A word passed as an identifier. The comparison is now a set of identifier- shaped tokens, and both cases are tests, so the reported bug and its near-miss are pinned. What you see now, five runs out of five: the same task comes back clarification_required · missing: order_id , and answering with the order proceeds into the manager approval it always needed. What the platform still does not do is check that an order exists before acting — that is the tool's job, and it is the next question."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"why-allowed","t":"Why did a wrong order come back allowed ?","x":"09 Why did a wrong order come back allowed ? Because \"allowed\" is policy's answer, not the tool's. Policy allowed the call — the amount was under the auto-approval limit — and the tool then discovered the order does not exist and said so. The screen showed policy's word and the tool's failure side by side, and \"allowed\" read as success. The amount is what decides it: Task What policy says What you saw order z, $200 the id is not an identifier at all → asks Which order? order o-9999, $200 $200 > $50 → held for approval Held — a manager decides order o-9999, $20 ≤ $50 → allowed , then the tool answers allowed + unknown order The outcome was deliberate, and I did not reverse it Before changing anything I found a test that says so explicitly: test_a_missing_record_is_a_result_not_an_error — \"asking for a record that is not there is an answer the caller can read, not a failure.\" A previous slice chose that on purpose. So the outcome stayed, and the two things genuinely wrong got fixed instead. The audit gap. The trail held only tool.allowed , which records the decision and not what came back — so \"did the refund actually happen?\" could not be answered from the audit at all. The tool's own answer is now recorded too: tool.reported_error , with the message. The display. \"allowed\" no longer stands alone: the chip reads allowed — but nothing happened , the step says the call was allowed and the tool answered unknown order , and the result block is headed The tool's answer . commands — the trail, on the running cluster tool.allowed {\"tool\": \"refunds.issue\", \"reason\": \"allowed: refunds.issue is within your role\"} tool.reported_error {\"tool\": \"refunds.issue\", \"reason\": \"unknown order\"} The honest limit that remains: an identifier that does not exist is something only the backend knows. The platform could check first — an extra read the person did not ask for, with its own policy question — and that is a design decision, not a bug fix."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"cloud","t":"What was the cloud-provider work?","x":"10 What was the cloud-provider work? Making the gateway work with any OpenAI-compatible provider — done (S17). Two defects in the hosted branch stopped it working, and the interesting half was a decision, not a code fix: may a cloud model see the personal data the agent can read under approval? The answer, recorded in ADR-0013, is that a cloud model is opt-in and the fine control belongs in policy. The default is still local (Ollama), deliberately: when the agent reads personal data under an approval, nothing leaves the machine. What the slice changed: Was Now The provider received no model and failed — the gateway forwarded the caller's request, and the agent sends none. The gateway puts the model it resolved (for the audit) into the forwarded request too, and fails clearly when none is configured. A strict provider could reject the formal JSON hint the agent sends. LLM_STRIP_RESPONSE_FORMAT=1 drops it; the agent also asks for JSON in words, so it still works. Testable without a cloud key setup.sh deploys a stub OpenAI-compatible provider in-cluster ( provider-stub ). Point the gateway at it — LLM_BASE_URL=http://provider-stub:8080/v1 — and a real run goes through the hosted branch; the stub is strict about both defects on purpose. A live run against a real provider is then only a matter of setting the key. The decision: what a cloud model may see Local-only means a run that reads PII under approval cannot send it anywhere. A cloud provider changes that — a multi-step run re-exposes the previous tool result to the model (T11). The platform's answer: a cloud provider is opt-in , and the fine-grained control (\"this tenant/tool may only use a local model\") belongs in policy , because the gateway cannot see which tool produced the prompt. Documented in ADR-0013; the policy rule itself is left as future work rather than pretended."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"multi","t":"What would many agents — and sub-agents — take?","x":"11 What would many agents — and sub-agents — take? There is no agent record anywhere. An agent exists by appearing in four registries owned by four different systems, and \"registration\" is a rule about when an identity may be issued — not a row about a robot. What Where it lives What is in it Identity SPIRE's datastore (its own Postgres database, durable) A registration entry: service account + namespace → SPIFFE ID. Today created imperatively by the setup script Delegation Keycloak's Postgres (the realm) A client whose clientId is the SPIFFE ID, authenticating with a JWT-SVID instead of a secret, with token exchange enabled and the audience/tenant mappers Admission Each service's configuration Which SPIFFE IDs may call it, and whose token exchange its user tokens may carry. Both default to the one agent Authorization The OPA bundle (policy in git, built and signed) Which agents are trusted, and the roles → tools matrix The gateway needs no change It accepts any caller holding a JWT-SVID audienced to it, and keys its rate and cost limits on the proven caller. A second agent would simply get its own budget — the multi-agent story there is already done. So a second agent is four edits SPIRE: give it a service account and a registration entry — the identity follows the workload. Keycloak: a client named by its SPIFFE ID, with the same three attributes and mappers. No secret: it signs with its JWT-SVID. Admission: add it to the allow-lists of the services it must reach. This is the knob that makes \"which agents may talk to which services\" a real decision — and the tool server additionally pins which agent's exchange its user tokens must come from. Policy: name it as trusted, and give it its own roles → tools matrix if it should differ. input.agent is already a policy input, so this is a natural extension. Sub-agents are the interesting case: a chain, not just an identity Today there is exactly one delegation hop — a person to the agent — using RFC 8693 token exchange. Agent A calling agent B needs three things, two of which are half-anticipated: Need State of play The chain must name the path (person → A → B) RFC 8693 expresses this with the act (actor) claim, and the SDK already reads it in preference to the token's issuer. What is missing is producing it: the exchange accepts extra parameters, but nothing passes an actor token yet. B must act as the same human , never as itself The subject stays the person's; A and B are added to the chain. A sub-agent acting purely as a service identity is a different thing and should not touch user data at all. A sub-agent must get less, not more The exchange already takes a scope, so a parent can hand down a narrowed one. Policy should then decide per agent, because \"may this agent refund for this user\" is a different question from \"may the agent\". The lifecycle of an agent Phase What happens Today Register Service account + registration entry + Keycloak client + admission lists + policy grant, reviewed works bu"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"kms","t":"Disk or KMS for the identity keys?","x":"12 Disk or KMS for the identity keys? Wherever SPIRE's KeyManager puts them — and that one setting decides whether the identity tier can be a failover pair. Every credential this platform issues is signed by a certificate authority, and that CA's private key has to live somewhere: on a volume, or inside a KMS. Two replicas can only serve the same trust bundle if they sign with the same CA. First, a distinction that trips people up: the registry — the attested agents and registration entries — is already in Postgres, and always is. That half of HA was S1's first half. What switches here is only the key manager : where the signing key lives. disk (default) aws_kms Where the CA key is A file on the server's volume ( /run/spire/data/keys.json ), on a PVC so a restart keeps the same CA Inside AWS KMS. Created, rotated and used there — the private key never leaves How many replicas One. The file cannot be shared, so two replicas would each mint their own CA — worse than a single server 2+ — every replica signs with the same key, so they serve the same bundle What it needs Nothing. No credentials, no network. This is why an unattended demo uses it An AWS account, a KMS key's worth of permissions, and credentials that reach AWS What a leaked credential means A file in your cluster reading a key in your cluster Still worth scoping: the recommended setup puts a role in front, so the long-lived credential can only assume it (below) Critical path Volume + one pod Volume + pod + AWS KMS reachability and credentials Both modes are blocks in one template, and the mode is a flag from the environment — so there is no second config file to drift: illustrative — the two blocks, from the SPIRE server config template KeyManager \"aws_kms\" { plugin_data { region = \"<region>\" key_identifier_value = \"<shared id>\" enable_tag_based_key_discovery = true } } KeyManager \"disk\" { plugin_data { keys_path = \"/run/spire/data/keys.json\" } } How to switch Both directions are the same two steps: say so in .env , then run the setup script. Only the content of the first step differs. commands — the KMS direction # 1. the AWS side, once: a user whose only power is to assume a role, and the role ./scripts/setup-aws-kms-role.sh --region eu-west-1 [--external-id] [--dry-run] # 2. .env — written by the script above where it can be; this is what it sets SPIRE_KEY_MANAGER=aws_kms SPIRE_KMS_REGION=eu-west-1 SPIRE_KMS_SERVER_ID=acme-com # MUST be identical on every replica, and no dots AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=... AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=... # 3. deploy it: renders the KMS block, stages the credentials, scales to 2 replicas, # and gates on both replicas presenting the same trust bundle ./scripts/setup.sh commands — back to disk # .env: SPIRE_KEY_MANAGER=disk — then the same command ./scripts/setup.sh # setup.sh also deletes the KMS credential Secret, key policy and config, so # switching back does not leave usable credentials in the cluster scripts/setup.sh KEY_MANAGER=\"${SPIRE_KEY_MANAGER:-disk}\" KMS_"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"policy","t":"How do I inspect the OPA policy and make edits?","x":"13 How do I inspect the OPA policy and make edits? Policy is code, and it lives in one file: policy/authz.rego (package agentnhi.authz ). The running engine does not read that file — it loads a bundle built from it and stamped with a revision. So there are three ways in: read the file, ask the live OPA, or read a decision's policy_version . The file, and the two layers worth knowing The role → tool matrix sits in two parts, on purpose: default_role_tools — the matrix for every tenant; tenant_role_tools — per-tenant overrides that replace the default for a role entirely. A role the tenant does not mention falls back to the default. That second layer is the S8 demonstration: the same role name with different power, decided by which customer you belong to. Inspect the running policy OPA answers a data API. Port-forward it and send an input document — the same shape the tool server sends: commands — ask the live policy for a decision kubectl -n agent-platform port-forward svc/opa 8181:8181 & curl -s localhost:8181/v1/data/agentnhi/authz \\ -d '{\"input\":{\"agent\":\"spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent\", \"user\":\"alice\",\"roles\":[\"support_rep\"],\"tenant\":\"acme\", \"tool\":\"refunds.issue\",\"amount\":200}}' and it answers with the decision, its reason, and the revision that produced it: illustrative — the reply, trimmed {\"decision\": \"require_approval\", \"reason\": \"requires manager approval: refund of 200 is above the auto-approval limit of 50\", \"policy_version\": \"1b8f8ff\"} Query /v1/data/agentnhi/authz/role_matrix instead for the whole matrix a tenant gets — the same thing the Roles & tools page shows. The two layers are visible in one line: the same role name, two tenants. illustrative — support_rep for acme vs globex support_rep, tenant \"acme\": crm.customer.read crm.orders.list refunds.issue refunds.quote tickets.read tickets.reply.draft support_rep, tenant \"globex\": crm.customer.read crm.orders.list refunds.quote tickets.read tickets.reply.draft Make an edit Change the file. Add a tool to a role in default_role_tools , or add a tenant override in tenant_role_tools . The policy file, not a data document, is deliberate: changing a customer's powers is then a reviewed, signed policy release. Add a test. policy/tests/authz_test.rego is the review surface. It has data-hygiene guards — an override may only name a real role and a real tool — so a typo fails the suite rather than quietly denying. Run the tests — the same command CI runs: commands — the policy suite docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/w -w /w openpolicyagent/opa:1.9.0 test policy/ # PASS: 45/45 Roll it out. ./scripts/setup.sh rebuilds the bundle (revision = the git short SHA), re-creates the opa-bundle ConfigMap, and restarts OPA . Every decision from then on reports the new revision. To choose the revision yourself: POLICY_REVISION=v42 ./scripts/build-bundle.sh . Verify. Query OPA again and watch policy_version change; ./scripts/role-tools.sh walks the matrix end to end; the audit event carries the revisi"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"rfc8693","t":"What is RFC 8693, and how does this project use it?","x":"14 What is RFC 8693, and how does this project use it? RFC 8693 is OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange : a client presents a token it holds and gets a new token back — usually narrower, for a different audience. It is the standard behind the one sentence this project turns on: the agent acts on the user's behalf without ever holding the user's credentials . The grant, in this project The whole exchange is one call to Keycloak's token endpoint, in sdk/agentnhi/exchange.py . The three URNs it needs: sdk/agentnhi/exchange.py TOKEN_EXCHANGE_GRANT = \"urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange\" ACCESS_TOKEN_TYPE = \"urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:access_token\" JWT_BEARER_ASSERTION_TYPE = \"urn:ietf:params:oauth:client-assertion-type:jwt-bearer\" and the request it sends: sdk/agentnhi/exchange.py data: dict[str, str] = { \"grant_type\": TOKEN_EXCHANGE_GRANT, \"client_id\": client_id, \"subject_token\": subject_token, \"subject_token_type\": ACCESS_TOKEN_TYPE, \"requested_token_type\": ACCESS_TOKEN_TYPE, } Read it as the RFC reads it: subject_token is the user's access token ( subject_token_type=access_token ), and the caller wants an access token back ( requested_token_type=access_token ). The caller authenticates as client_id — here, the agent's SPIFFE ID — with a client_assertion : the agent's JWT-SVID, presented as jwt-bearer . There is no client secret; the workload's own identity is the credential. The demo lets Keycloak's audience mapper set the result's aud , which is how Standard Token Exchange V2 expresses authorization (ADR-0002). What comes back, and why it is \"delegation\" The result is a token that still names the human as its subject, but records the agent as the actor and is audienced to exactly one service: Claim Value here What it means sub the support rep The human it is for azp the agent's SPIFFE ID The workload acting on their behalf aud the tool server The only place the token is valid That is the RFC's delegation model: the subject is preserved and the actor is recorded. RFC 8693 names the actor in an act claim; the SDK reads that if present and falls back to Keycloak's azp : sdk/agentnhi/tokens.py act = claims.get(\"act\") or {} workload = act.get(\"sub\") or claims.get(\"azp\") or \"?\" What the tool server checks — and what a forwarded token fails The resource server verifies three things ( TokenVerifier ): signature and issuer; the audience , which must be this service — that is what makes a token captured for one service useless at another; and the issued-to ( azp ), which must be the workload we expect. So the exchange is not a nicety: it is why the agent's token cannot be replayed, and why no hop forwards the token it received. Two shapes the SDK supports Client assertion — a workload proving identity with its SVID. This is the agent's exchange, and the only one live in the demo. Client secret — a service exchanging for a downstream audience. The SDK supports it for a real vendor integration; the demo's sandbox is a vendor stand-in scoped by an X-Ten"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"mcp","t":"If the tools were replaced with MCP, how would that be different?","x":"15 If the tools were replaced with MCP, how would that be different? Less than it sounds. MCP is a transport and discovery standard, not an authorization model — so the part that decides who may do what would not move. What changes is how a tool is described, how a call travels on the wire, and where a \"no\" or a \"needs a human\" is read from. The one genuinely new capability is federation : MCP lets you add tool servers you do not own, and that is where the trust boundary would need a decision. It is already the decision — and half-built This is not an undecided what-if: ADR-0005 decided that every tool should be exposed as an MCP server, keeping the tool server as the policy enforcement point. The code already honors that — app/tools/mcp_server.py exposes the same catalogue over MCP, delegating to the identical enforcer — and there is a test that asserts it ( test_mcp_server.py ). app/tools/http_app.py \"\"\"HTTP transport for the tools — the Phase 1 interface. The MCP transport (app/tools/mcp_server.py) exposes the same tools over the Model Context Protocol; both delegate to the identical enforcement core, so the security behaviour is the same either way. \"\"\" What is not in place is the wiring. The running tools service serves app.tools.http_app:app , and build_mcp_asgi_app is never mounted anywhere — so the live agent still calls the HTTP transport, one POST per tool: app/agent/live.py token = self._tool_token() client, workload_headers = hop.open_hop(self._tool_server_url, self._tools_id) try: resp = client.post( f\"{self._tool_server_url}/tools/{tool}\", json=body, headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\", **workload_headers}, timeout=30, ) So \"replacing tools with MCP\" is mostly swapping that call for a JSON-RPC tools/call against a transport that already exists — not rebuilding the tools. What changes, and what does not Concern Today — HTTP transport With MCP Wire POST /tools/<name> JSON-RPC tools/call Discovery the agent builds a prompt manifest, _tool_manifest tools/list returns schemas Schemas declared by hand in catalog.py inferred from the typed handler A denial HTTP 403 a result with \"status\": \"denied\" A hold for approval HTTP 428 a result with \"status\": \"approval_required\" Who may call policy, in the enforcer policy, in the enforcer Token check ( aud + azp ) in the enforcer unchanged Approval check, audit, own token exchange in the enforcer unchanged The row that carries the real interface difference is the outcome mapping. Over HTTP, the transport turns the enforcer's outcome into a status code the caller reads: app/tools/http_app.py if result.outcome is Outcome.OK: return {\"tool\": result.tool, \"decision\": result.decision, \"result\": result.result} if result.outcome is Outcome.DENIED: raise HTTPException(status_code=403, detail=result.reason) if result.outcome is Outcome.APPROVAL_REQUIRED: # 428 Precondition Required: the action is valid but needs a human. raise HTTPException(status_code=428, detail=result.reason) raise HTTPException(s"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"admin-create","t":"When the admin creates a user, does it land in Keycloak?","x":"16 When the admin creates a user, does it land in Keycloak? Yes — Keycloak is the user store. The API keeps no user table of its own: the admin's \"create user\" is a write to Keycloak's Admin API, and the id it returns is Keycloak's id. The endpoint calls Keycloak POST /admin/users is gated on platform_admin , then hands the work to the identity admin built from the environment: app/api/admin.py @router.post(\"/users\") def create_user(body: dict, admin_dep: Delegation = Depends(require_admin)) -> dict: username = (body.get(\"username\") or \"\").strip() password = body.get(\"password\") or \"\" if not username or not password: raise HTTPException(status_code=400, detail=\"username and password are required\") roles = [r for r in (body.get(\"roles\") or []) if r in ASSIGNABLE_ROLES] admin = admin_from_env() IdentityAdmin.create_user then POST s to Keycloak's /users . The payload carries the username, email, an enabled flag, the password as a credential, and — if a tenant was named — the tenant as an identity attribute. A duplicate comes back as Keycloak's 409 , which the API turns into \"that username is already taken\": app/api/identity.py if tenant: # The tenant is an identity attribute, mapped into the token by the # realm's `tenant` protocol mapper. Without it the account is # unscoped and policy denies everything. payload[\"attributes\"] = {\"tenant\": [tenant]} resp = self._request(\"POST\", \"/users\", json=payload) if resp.status_code == 409: raise ValueError(\"that username is already taken\") resp.raise_for_status() Which credential writes it Not the master admin . The API authenticates as a least-privilege platform-admin service account over client-credentials — it holds manage-users and read-roles on the realm, and nothing else: app/api/identity.py def admin_from_env() -> IdentityAdmin: settings = Settings.from_env() realm = settings.keycloak_issuer.rstrip(\"/\") return IdentityAdmin( admin_url=os.environ.get(\"KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_URL\", realm.replace(\"/realms/\", \"/admin/realms/\")), issuer=realm, client_id=os.environ.get(\"ADMIN_CLIENT_ID\", \"platform-admin\"), client_secret=os.environ.get(\"ADMIN_CLIENT_SECRET\", \"\"), ) The same is true of everything else about a user Granting a role ( POST /admin/users/{id}/roles ), revoking one, enabling or disabling an account, and resetting a password are all writes to Keycloak's Admin API — as is self-service enrollment ( POST /enroll ), which creates the same record with no role. The app never keeps a copy, which is why there is nothing to sync and nothing that can drift. See it for yourself ./scripts/show-keycloak-users.sh lists the realm straight from Keycloak's Admin API ( Q3 ) — an admin-created user appears there because that is where it was written."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"show-policy","t":"How do I show someone the policy-as-code (OPA) side?","x":"17 How do I show someone the policy-as-code (OPA) side? Show it the way you would any code: read the rule, run its tests, then run it for real. One file and two commands prove the three things that matter — the rules are code, they are tested , and they are what actually decides . Q13 is the deep dive (querying and editing); this is the five-minute demonstration. 1 · It is a file, and the file is the rule Open policy/authz.rego (package agentnhi.authz ). The top states the contract — exactly what a decision is made from: policy/authz.rego # Input contract: # input.agent the acting workload's SPIFFE ID (from the token's azp) # input.user the human the action is for # input.roles the human's roles, e.g. [\"support_rep\"] # input.tenant the human's tenant # input.tool the tool being called # input.amount the refund amount (refund tools only) # # Output: # decision \"allow\" | \"deny\" | \"require_approval\" # reason one human-readable string (an `else` chain, so never a set) The resolution is three lines of precedence and a default that is deny — the single fact that makes \"the agent is allowed\" mean something: policy/authz.rego default decision := \"deny\" decision := \"deny\" if deny decision := \"require_approval\" if { needs_approval not deny } decision := \"allow\" if { allow not deny not needs_approval } 2 · It has tests — run them, in front of them This is the beat that lands. Policy is not prose in a wiki; it has a suite, and the suite is the review surface. policy/tests/authz_test.rego even has data-hygiene guards — an override may only name a real role and a real tool — so a typo fails the build rather than quietly denying at runtime. commands — the policy suite, the same command CI runs docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/w -w /w openpolicyagent/opa:1.9.0 test policy/ # PASS: 45/45 3 · Run it live — the decision names its rule Then ask the running engine the same question a tool server asks. Port-forward OPA and send an input document (the full mechanics are in Q13 ); the reply is the decision, the human-readable reason, and the revision that produced it: commands — ask the live policy for a decision kubectl -n agent-platform port-forward svc/opa 8181:8181 & curl -s localhost:8181/v1/data/agentnhi/authz -d '{\"input\":{ \"agent\":\"spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent\",\"user\":\"alice\", \"roles\":[\"support_rep\"],\"tenant\":\"acme\",\"tool\":\"refunds.issue\",\"amount\":200}}' illustrative — the reply, trimmed {\"decision\":\"require_approval\", \"reason\":\"requires manager approval: refund of 200 is above the auto-approval limit of 50\", \"policy_version\":\"1b8f8ff\"} Change one field — \"amount\": 25 — and the same caller is allowed ; change the role or the tenant and it may be denied . Nothing was redeployed; the rules decided. 4 · The punchline: the same role, two tenants Query /v1/data/agentnhi/authz/role_matrix , or just open the Roles & tools page in the UI (it renders this same table from the policy), and show support_rep in acme beside support_rep in globex : the same role name"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"prometheus","t":"How do I show someone the Prometheus UI?","x":"18 How do I show someone the Prometheus UI? One port-forward, then open http://localhost:9090 . Prometheus is not exposed at the edge — like the Keycloak console, Grafana and Jaeger, it is reached from your own machine, not through the ingress. commands — forward Prometheus and open it kubectl -n agent-platform port-forward svc/prometheus 9090:9090 # open http://localhost:9090 Run a little traffic first — a couple of sign-ins and one task, or ./scripts/demo.sh — so the counters are not zero. What to show, in order Status → Targets. Four targets, all up : the api twice — once per pod — plus opa and otel-collector . The api is scraped per pod on purpose: its counters are per-process, so scraping through the Service would round-robin between replicas and make rate() meaningless. The Graph tab. The platform's own counters are the interesting ones: Query What it answers up is every scrape target alive right now agent_platform_logins_total{result} logins, success vs failure agent_platform_http_requests_total request rates by method, route and status agent_platform_approvals_pending the approval backlog — read from the store at scrape time, so every replica reports the same queue agent_platform_audit_events_total{event} policy decisions and admin actions, derived from the audit stream The audit-derived counters are the point: every service forwards its audit events to the api, so one instrumentation point covers policy decisions across the whole platform — the numbers and the audit log cannot drift apart. The same data without a browser — the query API is what Grafana itself calls: commands — a raw query curl -s 'localhost:9090/api/v1/query?query=agent_platform_approvals_pending' Prometheus is the engine; Grafana is the dashboard Prometheus scrapes, stores, and answers queries. Grafana ( localhost:3000 ) draws the provisioned \"Agent Identity Platform\" dashboard on top of it, and Jaeger ( localhost:16686 ) holds the traces. All three are port-forwards, none is at the edge: kubectl -n agent-platform port-forward svc/grafana 3000:3000 and svc/jaeger 16686:16686 ."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"session","t":"Do users have to sign in every five minutes?","x":"19 Do users have to sign in every five minutes? Not any more. The access token still lives five minutes — that is a security property, not a bug — but the API now hands back a refresh token too, and the UI renews the token silently in the background. So an active tab is not thrown back to sign-in every five minutes. But it cannot stay signed in forever either: the session ends on two clocks, both Keycloak's. Before this change it was just the first one, with no renewal: a five-minute token and a forced sign-in. The two clocks that end it Keycloak owns both. The UI watches the first (the API returns it as refresh_expires_at ) and gives up the moment a renewal fails. Clock Setting Value What it means Idle ssoSessionIdleTimeout 30 min the refresh token's own exp ( iat + 1800 ). It slides on every renewal — a fresh refresh token with a fresh window — so a tab in use keeps going. Absolute ssoSessionMaxLifespan 10 h a ceiling measured from the original sign-in that renewal cannot push past. However active the session, at ten hours it must be re-authenticated. Why the access token stays short Revocation and role changes take effect within one token lifetime, so a token that leaks is useless quickly ( accessTokenLifespan: 300 in the realm). Lengthening it to avoid re-login would trade that away. Renewal keeps both: the token stays short, the session does not. How the renewal works POST /auth/login returns the session plus a refresh_token and refresh_expires_at . Before the access token expires, the UI calls POST /auth/refresh , which exchanges the refresh token at Keycloak for a new access token — server-side, with no password and no client secret in the browser: app/api/auth.py data={ \"grant_type\": \"refresh_token\", \"client_id\": os.environ.get(\"PORTAL_CLIENT_ID\", \"portal\"), \"client_secret\": os.environ.get(\"PORTAL_SECRET\", \"\"), \"refresh_token\": refresh_token, }, Keycloak issues a fresh refresh token on every use, so the newest one is stored each time. The UI renews about thirty seconds before expiry, and a request that still catches an expired token renews and retries once before giving up — so a page reload after five idle minutes comes back signed in, not on the sign-in screen. What still ends the session The idle window passing (30 minutes with no renewal), the 10-hour absolute ceiling being reached, the session being revoked, or an explicit sign-out. Then the UI says so and returns you to sign-in rather than showing you empty tables. In production the browser would use authorization-code + PKCE and keep the refresh token in an httpOnly cookie; here the API does the password grant server-side and the browser holds both tokens in sessionStorage — the same trust boundary, and it dies with the tab. One hardening step for production: enable Keycloak's revoke-refresh-token , so the previous refresh token is invalidated on use — this local realm issues a fresh one but leaves the old valid until it expires."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"agent-lifecycle","t":"What is the lifecycle of an agent?","x":"20 What is the lifecycle of an agent? There is no agent object with a lifecycle of its own — no row, no state on a disk. Two things have lifecycles: its identity (a SPIRE entry and the SVIDs issued from it) and each run (the graph state of one task). Both are short-lived on purpose, and neither is a credential kept in a file. Q11 lists the four registries an agent appears in; this is what happens to it over time. The identity: register → attest → work → rotate → restart → retire Register. A SPIRE registration entry means \"a workload with this service account in this namespace may be this SPIFFE ID\". It lives in SPIRE's Postgres (durable), created by setup.sh — and one entry per attested SPIRE agent , because a pod is attested by the agent on its own node: scripts/setup.sh for parent in $($SPIRE agent list -socketPath \"$SOCKET\" 2>/dev/null \\ | awk '/SPIFFE ID/{print $NF}'); do $SPIRE entry create -socketPath \"$SOCKET\" \\ -spiffeID \"$id\" -parentID \"$parent\" \\ -selector \"k8s:ns:$NS\" -selector \"k8s:sa:$sa\" >/dev/null done Attest. The node's SPIRE agent proves itself with k8s_psat (a projected service-account token the server checks through TokenReview). The workload then asks the Workload API over the socket and is matched by those selectors. It receives an X.509-SVID (1 h) and a JWT-SVID (5 m) — \"demo-tuned lifetimes: short SVIDs make rotation visible\" ( deploy/kind/manifests/spire/server.conf.tmpl , CA 24 h). Work. Per call, not per process: the agent fetches a JWT-SVID audienced to the gateway, and exchanges the user's token for the tool call. It holds no static credential — no client secret, no provider key. Rotate. SPIRE rotates an SVID at roughly half its life. A process that is serving one exits about two minutes before its own certificate expires, so it restarts holding a fresh one — read from the certificate, never assumed from the TTL: app/common/server.py def restart_before_expiry(cert_file: str, margin: int, service: str) -> None: \"\"\"Exit `margin` seconds before the certificate we are serving expires. The deadline comes from the certificate, not a constant. An interval that assumes a full lifetime serves an **expired** certificate for the difference, and every handshake fails until the process happens to restart (S15). Heal. Every container carries a liveness probe, so a hung process is restarted rather than reported forever; two replicas with anti-affinity and a PodDisruptionBudget mean a restart or an eviction is not an outage. Retire. Delete the workload and no new SVID is issued — an already-issued one stays valid only until it expires. But nothing removes the registration entry , and nothing removes the Keycloak client whose clientId is the SPIFFE ID. Deregistration is manual: the demo has the create half, not the destroy half. That last step is the honest gap Spinning an agent up is four registries ( Q11 ); taking one down is not automated at all. A retired agent leaves its entry and its client behind — harmless once the workload is "},{"p":"questions.html","a":"revoke","t":"Can a rogue agent be revoked?","x":"21 Can a rogue agent be revoked? Yes, but only as \"stop issuing and kill the holder\" — there is no un-issue. An SVID is a bearer credential with no CRL or OCSP, so a copied one works until it expires. The platform can stop the identity being renewed, can refuse it at every hop, and can kill the process that holds it; it cannot reach out and invalidate a certificate already in someone's hands. Today this is a manual runbook, and a first-class capability is backlogged as S23 . First, \"rogue\" is two different problems An identity that should never have existed — a workload that is not the registered service account. SPIRE issues it nothing (attack suite #1), so there is nothing to revoke. A legitimate agent gone bad — the trusted SPIFFE ID itself. This is the hard case: policy cannot tell it from the good one, because it is the identity policy trusts. You remove trust, not a stranger. The trust points, and the lever at each Trust point Lever Latency Identity issuance — the SPIRE entry (namespace + service account → SPIFFE ID) spire-server entry delete immediate for new SVIDs The held credential — X.509-SVID (1 h) or JWT-SVID (5 m) delete the pod, or wait out the TTL bounded by the TTL Admission — ALLOWED_WORKLOADS ( app/common/workload.py ; default: the one agent) remove the SPIFFE ID and restart the service next call, no TTL Authorization — OPA is_trusted / input.agent edit the policy, rebuild the bundle, restart OPA minutes Delegation — the Keycloak client whose clientId is the SPIFFE ID disable the client: token exchange fails immediate (manual) Model access — the gateway none : it accepts any valid JWT-SVID audienced to it only the 5 m TTL What the runbook is today docs/operator-guide.md §5.B: delete the registration entry so the SVID cannot be renewed, then delete the pod so the current one stops being presented. commands — the operator runbook, manual SPIRE=\"kubectl -n agent-platform exec spire-server-0 -- /opt/spire/bin/spire-server\" SOCKET=/run/spire/server/private/api.sock $SPIRE entry show -socketPath $SOCKET -spiffeID <spiffe-id> $SPIRE entry delete -socketPath $SOCKET -entryID <entry-id> kubectl -n agent-platform delete pod -l app=agent The honest limits No CRL/OCSP, so a copied SVID key lives until it expires. No central or automated revoke — only user revocation is scripted ( POST /admin/users/{id}/enabled , revoke a role). The gateway does not name-check callers. And nothing reaps the SPIRE entry or the Keycloak client when a workload is retired ( Q20 's deregistration gap). Closing these — a runbook wrapper, an admission denylist fed by the api, or a policy/mesh decision — is S23 in docs/backlog.md , deliberately deferred."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"inspect","t":"How do I inspect every component?","x":"22 How do I inspect every component? Through one of three doors — and the component decides which. Only the API is published (the https edge); everything else is reached by kubectl port-forward to your own machine, or by kubectl exec inside the pod — and for a few pieces (SPIRE, Postgres, the model) a CLI inside is the only view. This is the index: one row per component, then the exact commands, grouped the way the system is. Why not just expose everything? The edge routes / to the API alone, so the Keycloak console, OPA, Prometheus, Grafana, Jaeger, the SPIRE API and Postgres have no route from outside. That is the security posture, not an omission — so an inspector works from the host (port-forward or exec). The one exception, the Keycloak console, needs a hosts entry because the realm pins its hostname to keycloak ( Q3 ). The whole system at a glance Component What it is How to look api our FastAPI + the UI the edge https://localhost:8443 ; kubectl logs deploy/api ; curl --cacert .edge/ca.crt …/audit web our React UI, served by the api the edge; tabs Console · Approvals · Privacy · Roles · Audit · Admin agent our LangGraph runtime ./logs.sh ; kubectl logs deploy/agent ; its runs live in Postgres tools our policy enforcement point ./scripts/role-tools.sh ; kubectl logs deploy/tools gateway our only egress to a model kubectl logs deploy/gateway | grep llm.call ; its limits are in Postgres sandbox our synthetic vendor systems a call to sandbox:8090 from the tools pod ( Q4 ) Keycloak identity, users, tokens ./scripts/show-keycloak-users.sh ; console on keycloak:8080 ( Q3 ) SPIRE server workload identity registry spire-server entry show / agent list / bundle show SPIRE agent attests pods, per node kubectl get pods -l app=spire-agent ; its logs OPA the policy decisions port-forward svc/opa 8181 ; /v1/data/agentnhi/authz ( Q13 ) Postgres everything durable exec deploy/postgres -- psql -U agent ; then \\dt otel-collector the trace/metric pipeline port-forward svc/otel-collector 8888 ; /metrics Prometheus metrics engine port-forward svc/prometheus 9090 ( Q18 ) Grafana the dashboard port-forward svc/grafana 3000 Jaeger the traces ./scripts/show-trace.sh ; port-forward svc/jaeger 16686 Ollama the model, on your host ollama list ; ./scripts/use-model.sh Linkerd the mesh (mTLS every hop) linkerd check ; ./scripts/tls-check.sh ingress the one published door kubectl -n ingress-nginx get pods ; ./start.sh HPA · adapter autoscaling on queue depth kubectl get hpa ; the raw external metric below Kyverno image-signature admission declared in the tree; not installed by the local setup commands — the first three things to run ./status.sh # cluster up? pods ready? the URL kubectl -n agent-platform get pods -o wide # every pod, its node, its restarts ./logs.sh # the audit stream across agent, tools, api, gateway Our services The four we wrote expose no console of their own — the UI is their console. For the three with no user-facing port, the log line and the audit ev"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"svid-client","t":"Is the agent's SVID the same as its Keycloak client_id ?","x":"23 Is the agent's SVID the same as its Keycloak client_id ? No — one is the name, the other is the proof. The client_id is the SPIFFE ID as a stable string in the realm; the SVID is a short-lived credential SPIRE mints for that identity. They are tied together — the JWT-SVID's subject is the same URI as the client_id — but the string is not the credential, and the credential is not stored anywhere. The client_id is the SPIFFE ID Keycloak's client for the agent is named by the identity itself. In the realm template ( deploy/kind/manifests/keycloak/realm.json.tmpl ), the client id is the SPIFFE ID: illustrative — the agent client in the realm template \"clientId\": \"spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent\", The SVID is the credential that proves it The agent fetches a fresh JWT-SVID and presents it to Keycloak as the client_assertion , naming that same SPIFFE ID as the client_id : app/agent/live.py svid = fetch_jwt_svid(self._settings.spiffe_socket, self._settings.keycloak_issuer) return TokenExchanger(self._settings).exchange( subject_token=self._user_token, client_id=self._agent_id, client_assertion=svid, ) Keycloak is configured to verify that assertion's signature against SPIRE's published keys rather than a shared secret — which is why no client secret exists: illustrative — the same client's authenticator, from the realm template \"clientAuthenticatorType\": \"client-jwt\", \"serviceAccountsEnabled\": true, \"standardFlowEnabled\": false, \"directAccessGrantsEnabled\": false, \"attributes\": { \"use.jwks.url\": \"true\", \"jwks.url\": \"http://spire-oidc-discovery.agent-platform.svc.cluster.local:11080/keys\", \"token.endpoint.auth.signing.alg\": \"ES256\", \"standard.token.exchange.enabled\": \"true\" }, The SPIFFE ID / client_id The SVID What it is a URI string naming the identity a signed credential proving it Where it lives the Keycloak realm — and the policy, and the admission lists nowhere: fetched from the Workload API and held in memory What changes it a registration decision; the same URI appears in four registries ( Q11 ) nothing — it rotates on its own How long it lasts stable until someone changes the registration JWT-SVID ~5 m; X.509-SVID 1 h ( Q20 ) What uses it the client_id on the exchange; the azp on the result the client_assertion ; the mTLS client cert on our hops The agent holds two SVIDs, and only one is the client assertion Both carry the same SPIFFE ID. The JWT-SVID is what Keycloak sees, as the client_assertion . The X.509-SVID is the mTLS client certificate on the SPIFFE hops to tools and gateway . Neither is a secret you provision; both expire on their own. After the exchange, look at azp The result token's azp is that same SPIFFE ID — the actor acting for the human — not portal or mcp-tools , which are separate clients ( Q14 ). The SDK reads the RFC 8693 act claim first and falls back to Keycloak's azp : sdk/agentnhi/tokens.py act = claims.get(\"act\") or {} workload = act.get(\"sub\") or claims.get(\"azp\") or \"?\" See it on the running cluster ."},{"p":"questions.html","a":"oauth","t":"Is there an OAuth flow, and where is it?","x":"24 Is there an OAuth flow, and where is it? Yes — four of them, and they are load-bearing. Keycloak is an OAuth 2.0 / OIDC authorization server, and the platform uses four grants, each for one job: password to sign a person in, refresh_token to keep the session alive, client_credentials for the API's own admin service account, and RFC 8693 token exchange for the delegation the whole platform turns on — the agent acting for the user without holding their credentials (Q14). The browser never talks to Keycloak: the API is the OAuth client, server-side. Each flow, drawn A box is an actor and an arrow is a call; the number on each arrow is that step in the grant. The colour is the same across this page — blue is the OAuth request itself, green is a response or a decision, violet is a token-endpoint or Admin API call, and amber is a call that reaches a service. The first four are the flows this code runs; the fifth is the one production would use instead of the password grant. Sign-in — the password grant browser the resource owner api the OAuth client (portal) Keycloak the authorization server 1 POST /auth/login username + password 4 session: tokens, roles, tools 2 grant_type=password · client_id=portal 3 access + refresh token The browser never talks to Keycloak. The API is the OAuth client, so the password reaches it — which is why production replaces this with authorization code + PKCE. Staying signed in — the refresh grant browser a live session api the OAuth client (portal) Keycloak the authorization server 1 POST /auth/refresh the refresh token 4 renewed session 2 grant_type=refresh_token · client_id=portal 3 new access + rotated refresh The access token still lives five minutes; this is what stops that from meaning “sign in again” (Q19). Keycloak rotates the refresh token on every use. The API acting as itself — client credentials api the platform-admin client Keycloak the authorization server Keycloak Admin API create user · grant role 1 grant_type=client_credentials client_id=platform-admin · client_secret 2 service-account token 3 POST /admin/realms/… — Bearer <token> This is the only grant with no person: it is the API managing users and roles in Keycloak (Q16). The service account holds manage-users and read-roles, nothing else. The delegation — RFC 8693 token exchange browser (via the api) the resource owner Keycloak the authorization server agent the client — holds no secret tools the resource server OPA policy 1 the user's token (aud=agent) 2 grant_type=token-exchange client_id = SPIFFE ID client_assertion = JWT-SVID 3 aud=mcp-tools · sub=user · azp=agent 4 Bearer <token> + X-Workload-Token <JWT-SVID> 5 policy question 6 allow | deny | require_approval This is the flow the platform turns on: the token's subject is the person, its actor is the agent, and it is valid at one service — so the agent acts for the user without ever holding their credentials (Q14). For comparison — what production would use Resource owner user + browser Authori"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"oidc-login","t":"So why are we not using OIDC for user login?","x":"25 So why are we not using OIDC for user login? We are using the OIDC provider — we are just not using the redirect. Keycloak is the OpenID Provider, the realm's clients speak openid-connect , and every service verifies tokens against the realm's issuer and JWKS. What the demo skips is the authorization-code flow — the browser round-trip to /authorize — that \"OIDC login\" usually means. Instead the realm turns the standard flow off and direct access grants on , and the API runs the password grant server-side. It is a deliberate demo choice, named in the code rather than hidden (Q19, Q24). What we use, and what we skip This platform A redirect OIDC login Provider Keycloak, realm agent-platform the same Token endpoint /protocol/openid-connect/token the same Key discovery JWKS at /protocol/openid-connect/certs ; issuer and audience checked the same, usually via /.well-known/openid-configuration Grant password (ROPC), run server-side in the API authorization code + PKCE, run in the browser Where the browser goes only the API — never Keycloak Keycloak's hosted login page, then back Token consumed the access token (roles, tenant) the access token and an ID token; often userinfo So the honest phrasing is: OAuth 2.0 password grant, against an OIDC-capable server, with OIDC's discovery and JWKS used for verification. We do not request an ID token and never call userinfo — the app reads identity and roles from the access token. Why the demo does it this way It keeps the demo self-contained. The browser only ever talks to the API, so there is no redirect URI to register and host, no route from the browser to Keycloak, no CORS or third-party-cookie surface — and it sidesteps the puzzle in Q3, where KC_HOSTNAME_STRICT pins the realm to the internal name keycloak . The ingress routes / to the API alone. The API is already the OAuth client. It holds the portal client secret and runs every other Keycloak call, so minting and verifying the session in one place is one fewer moving part — and the token can be issued with both audiences in a single place. It is documented as a demo limitation, not dressed up as a design win. The password grant is removed in OAuth 2.1, and the pages say so and draw the replacement (Q24, flow 5). The realm says it out loud The realm template ( deploy/kind/manifests/keycloak/realm.json.tmpl ) turns the standard flow off and direct access grants on for the portal client: illustrative — the portal client in the realm template \"directAccessGrantsEnabled\": true, \"standardFlowEnabled\": false, app/api/auth.py — the login grant data={ \"grant_type\": \"password\", \"client_id\": os.environ.get(\"PORTAL_CLIENT_ID\", \"portal\"), \"client_secret\": os.environ.get(\"PORTAL_SECRET\", \"\"), \"username\": username, \"password\": password, }, And the verification is ordinary OIDC/OAuth: the JWKS URL is built from the realm issuer, and jwt.decode checks the signature, the audience and the issuer: sdk/agentnhi/config.py def jwks_url(self) -> str: return f\"{self.keycloak_"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"jwt-svid-assertion","t":"Why does the JWT-SVID need a jti — and does the agent still use X.509?","x":"26 Why does the JWT-SVID need a jti — and does the agent still use X.509? Because the JWT-SVID is being used as an OAuth client assertion, not just as a bearer token. When a client authenticates by signing a JWT — private_key_jwt , the method RFC 7523 profiles — the authorization server uses the jti claim to detect a replay, and Keycloak requires it. SPIRE's JWT-SVIDs carry no jti , so this project adds one. And yes, the agent also uses its X.509-SVID, for a different job: the two SVIDs are not alternatives. The sentence, one clause at a time Phrase What it means client assertion Instead of a client secret , the client sends a signed JWT as proof of identity, in two form fields: client_assertion_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:client-assertion-type:jwt-bearer and client_assertion=<jwt> . private_key_jwt The OpenID Connect name for that method when the JWT is signed with the client's private key — the server verifies it against the client's registered public keys (a JWKS). Keycloak calls the authenticator client-jwt . RFC 7523 The spec that defines this JWT-as-assertion shape and the jti (JWT ID) claim: a unique id per assertion, so the server can remember what it has seen and refuse a second use. Replay protection, not decoration. SPIRE does not add one A SPIRE JWT-SVID carries iss , sub , aud , exp , iat — and nothing else. No jti . What SPIRE mints, and what Keycloak demands The agent asks the Workload API for a JWT-SVID audienced to the realm. By default the decoded assertion is missing the one claim Keycloak's client-JWT validator insists on: illustrative — a SPIRE JWT-SVID as it comes, decoded (no jti) { \"iss\": \"spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent\", \"sub\": \"spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent\", \"aud\": \"http://keycloak:8080/realms/agent-platform\", \"exp\": 1789954300, \"iat\": 1789954000 } illustrative — the same assertion with the jti this project adds at issuance { \"iss\": \"spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent\", \"sub\": \"spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent\", \"aud\": \"http://keycloak:8080/realms/agent-platform\", \"jti\": \"9f2c1d0b7a4e4f8e9c1a2b3d4e5f6071\", \"exp\": 1789954300, \"iat\": 1789954000 } Two claims carry the identity, and the demo makes them line up Keycloak's client-JWT validator is happiest when the assertion's iss equals its sub equals the client's client_id . The client id is the SPIFFE ID and the JWT-SVID's sub already is, so the demo sets SPIRE's jwt_issuer to the same value — the one place that tidies the demo, flagged in the config and removed by an authorization server that understands SPIFFE issuers natively (Q23, Q14). The exchange this assertion travels in illustrative — the token-exchange request body, one assertion per call POST /realms/agent-platform/protocol/openid-connect/token Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange client_id=spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent client_assertion_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:client-assertion-"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"in-cluster","t":"Does everything run in the cluster?","x":"27 Does everything run in the cluster? Everything except the model. The synthetic systems, the identity system, policy, the durable database, the observability stack, the mesh and the edge all run inside the kind cluster — three nodes. The model is the one deliberate outside dependency: it runs on the host , and only the gateway can reach it. HOST — your machine (outside the cluster) kind cluster — 3 nodes (1 control-plane + 2 workers) namespace: agent-platform Ollama the model · host.docker.internal:11434 browser the UI + the edge CA api login · tasks · audit agent LangGraph · no secrets tools the enforcement point gateway the only model route sandbox synthetic vendor keycloak idP spire-server workload IDs spire-agent attests pods opa policy postgres durable state otel-collector pipeline prometheus metrics grafana dashboard jaeger traces prom-adapter HPA metrics the third-party services run here too, in the same namespace ingress-nginx the edge · terminates TLS linkerd (+ viz) the mesh kube-system coredns · metrics-server · control plane every hop is authenticated — SPIFFE between our services, the mesh for the third-party ones https :8443 host.docker.internal:11434 — the one thing that leaves the cluster Boxes are services; the dashed border is your machine. Everything is inside the kind cluster except the browser and the model — and only the gateway has a route to the model. What runs where Layer In the cluster Our services api · agent · tools · gateway (two each) and sandbox Identity keycloak ×2 (and the realm-import Job), spire-server and spire-agent ×2 Policy opa ×2 Data postgres ×1 Observability otel-collector , prometheus , grafana , jaeger , prometheus-adapter Edge ingress-nginx-controller Mesh Linkerd control plane ( destination , identity , proxy-injector ) and linkerd-viz Autoscaling meter metrics-server (kube-system) Cluster plumbing coredns, kindnet, kube-proxy, local-path-provisioner, the control plane The model is the exception, on purpose Ollama runs on the host at http://host.docker.internal:11434 ( qwen3-warden-ctx16k:latest ). The gateway holds the only route to it, which is what lets the platform keep its promise that the model proposes and the platform disposes — and why a local model by default sends nothing off the machine (Q10, Q24). See it yourself commands — every pod, in every namespace kubectl get pods -A kubectl get nodes # three kind nodes: one control-plane, two workers Your browser and the edge's CA ( .edge/ca.crt ) live on the host too, but those are the client and the trust anchor, not services. The kind nodes themselves are Docker containers on the host — that is the cluster runtime, not a dependency. One box the kind cluster leaves out: Kyverno The image-signature admission policy is declared in the repo ( deploy/kind/manifests/supply-chain/ ) and exercised in CI, but the local demo does not install Kyverno, so nothing enforces signatures here. It is the one thing in the architecture that is not running locall"},{"p":"questions.html","a":"downstream-identity","t":"What does the tool server send the simulated system?","x":"28 What does the tool server send the simulated system? No JWT — so no, the user is not the sub any more. The user's delegated token stops at the tool server . That server reads the identity and the tenant off the verified token and then calls the simulated system as a plain tenant-scoped request: an X-Tenant header (and, for a real vendor, an optional static bearer). The person's sub never crosses that boundary. Who appears where Hop Credential on the wire Whose identity Is the person present? agent → tool server a delegated OAuth access token ( Authorization: Bearer ) plus the agent's JWT-SVID in X-Workload-Token the user ( sub ) and the agent ( azp ) yes — sub is the human tool server → simulated system X-Tenant: acme (and, if configured, a static SANDBOX_TOKEN bearer) nobody — a tenant, not a person no — there is no token to carry a sub The token that does carry the user — the delegated one This is what the agent presents to the tool server. Keycloak mints it in the RFC 8693 exchange (Q14): the sub is the human, the azp is the agent, and it is audienced to exactly one service. illustrative — the delegated access token, decoded { \"iss\": \"http://keycloak:8080/realms/agent-platform\", \"sub\": \"b1a4…-uuid\", // the human (alice) \"preferred_username\": \"alice\", \"azp\": \"spiffe://acme.com/ns/agent-platform/sa/agent\", // the actor \"aud\": \"mcp-tools\", // only the tool server accepts it \"tenant\": \"acme\", \"realm_access\": { \"roles\": [\"support_rep\"] }, \"exp\": 1790954300, \"iat\": 1790954000, \"jti\": \"6f0c…\", \"typ\": \"Bearer\" } The X-Workload-Token sent alongside it is a different JWT — a SPIFFE JWT-SVID whose sub is the agent's SPIFFE ID, proving which workload is calling (Q23, Q26). It names the agent, not the person. What actually reaches the simulated system illustrative — the tool server's request to the sandbox POST /orders/o-1001/refunds X-Tenant: acme Authorization: Bearer <SANDBOX_TOKEN> # empty in the demo — header omitted Content-Type: application/json {\"amount\": 200, \"idempotency_key\": \"ap-344aed0d29f1\"} The HTTP backend builds exactly that — the tenant is the only identity that goes on the wire, and the static token is added only if it is set (it is not, in the demo): app/tools/backends.py def _request(self, method: str, path: str, tenant: str, **kwargs) -> Any: headers = {\"X-Tenant\": tenant} if self._token: headers[\"Authorization\"] = f\"Bearer {self._token}\" On the receiving side the simulated system refuses anything without a tenant, and takes it from the header — there is no token to read a sub from: app/sandbox/app.py def _tenant(x_tenant: str | None) -> str: if not x_tenant: raise HTTPException(status_code=400, detail=\"X-Tenant header is required\") return x_tenant Why the tenant is safe to trust Because it is not taken from the request. The tool server derives it from the verified token and hands it to the handler; the caller cannot name a tenant it does not belong to: app/tools/enforcement.py # The tenant comes from the identity, never from the c"}];
